## 2.5 The T-Series Substrate (Added v0.2.5)

A parallel program of T-series preprints (T0–T14, Murillo Montero 2026) has,
in the weeks following draft 0.2.4 of this paper, rigorized in independent
machinery the arithmetic–geometric substrate Paper 32 uses. The T-series sits
*structurally upstream* of Papers 13–28 (its content was implicit throughout
the simplectic-mechanics arc) but its *explicit* form arrives only now. The
discharge effect on Paper 32 is uniformly positive and uniformly anchor-only:
every load-bearing claim acquires an independently formulated external
foundation; no tier is upgraded by this section alone, and no new conjecture
opens. The contribution is the explicit recording of where each claim of
Paper 32 now lives in the T-series substrate, plus four new observations
(§2.5.1–§2.5.4) the substrate immediately yields. **T11 + T12 + T13 + T14 close
the four structural axes of the framework — T11 closes the
character/conductor axis (§2.5.6), T12 closes the order/tensor axis
(§2.5.6b), T13 closes the projection / RG-flow axis (§2.5.6c), and
**T14 closes the geometry / curvature axis (§2.5.6d)**. The
framework is now finite arithmetic perturbation theory with no
algebraically free degrees of freedom beyond a finite combinatorial
structure (conductor lattice, packet decomposition, closure operator
\(\operatorname{Cl}_n\), apex closure polynomial \(P_x\)), and **the
projected curvature, RG-source, Noether-defect, and beta-source are
all one finite object — the conductor-fusion boundary $\partial_n S$ —
read in different languages (T14 Thm 6, capstone)**.

