### 2.5.1 The Bures lift is the canonical complex-amplitude lift (T7)

T7 §1 Theorem 1.1 proves that Born closure
\(\pi_{\text{Born}}:\mathbb C^N\setminus\{0\}\to\Delta^{N-1}\) factors uniquely
as a three-layer composition
\[
\mathbb C^N\setminus\{0\}
\xrightarrow{\text{rad}} S^{2N-1}
\xrightarrow{\text{Hopf}} \mathbb{CP}^{N-1}
\xrightarrow{\mu_{\text{toric}}} \Delta^{N-1},
\]
quotienting in order by positive scaling \(\mathbb R_{>0}^\times\), the global
phase \(U(1)\), and the relative-phase torus \(T^{N-1}=U(1)^N/U(1)\). The
decoherent face \(S^{N-1}_+\subset S^{2N-1}\) (Paper 13's Hellinger embedding;
T1 §3) is a *section* of all three quotients.

**Consequence for §9.** The Bures lift of Paper 18 used throughout §9 is
*precisely* this \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) lift; the Bures metric (Paper 18 §6
SLD/Bures selector) is the Fubini–Study metric pulled back to the
density-matrix manifold \(D(V)\) at pure states, extended to mixed states by
the standard SLD construction. Two consequences hold immediately and
without new analytic work:

*(a) Cor 9.7 Part 1 (\(c\equiv 1\) is structurally unity) gains a uniqueness
anchor.* T7 §2 Theorem 2.1 establishes that the Fubini–Study metric is the
*unique* (up to scale) \(U(N)\)-invariant Kähler metric on \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\),
which restricts on the decoherent face to (one quarter of) the Fisher–Rao
metric. The statement "two faces of one \(\Phi\) admit no independent
normalization" of Cor 9.7 Part 1 is therefore the categorical-simplex
restriction of a uniqueness theorem on the natural complex-projective lift.
The factor of \(2\) in \(d_{FR}=2\arccos\sum\sqrt{p_iq_i}\) (T1 §4) is the
Hopf-fibration angle-doubling — the \(SU(2)\to SO(3)\) double cover at
\(N=2\). The "no separate ruler and no separate clock" of Cor 9.7 Part 1 is
the same statement read at the apex of the Hopf fibration.

*(b) Cor 9.7 Part 2 / §13.2A local-Casimir Lorentzian sub-bundle gains a
structural source.* The tangent space to \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) at the uniform
\([\psi(u)]\) is the complexification \(H_0\otimes\mathbb C\); the Casimir
quadratic \(\langle H\rangle^2-\sum_a\langle P_a\rangle^2\) of §13.2A is the
Hessian at the uniform of a quadratic functional on the same tangent space
that T7 §1.3 identifies as the decoherent-face section's complement
direction. The \((1,3,4)\) signature found in §13.2A's audit is *consistent*
with the Fubini–Study Kähler structure when the four
\(\mathfrak{su}(3)\)-generators \((H,P_a)\) are HS-orthogonal: the Lorentzian
\((1,3)\) sub-bundle is the "orthogonal direction × time"-block of the
Bures/Kähler metric.

T7's modulus-restriction map \(\mathcal R:z\mapsto|z|\) (T7 §4.1) is the
operational decoherence channel of Paper 32 §9.6 / Cor 9.9 read in
amplitudes: it sends every \(z\in\Omega_N\) to its modulus \(|z|\in\mathbb
R_{>0}^N\), which then re-embeds as zero-phase amplitudes on the decoherent
face. T7 §4.2 Theorem 4.1 establishes that this operation reduces the
complex apex generator \(\mathcal M_z(\beta,c)\) to T0's real-positive apex
\(\mathcal M_{|z|}(\beta,c)\). The dephasing channel of Cor 9.9
(diagonal/measurement projection \(\mathcal P:\rho\mapsto\sum_s\langle
s|\rho|s\rangle|s\rangle\langle s|\)) is the density-matrix version (T7
§4.5): on pure states \(\rho=|z\rangle\langle z|/\|z\|^2\), \(\mathcal
P(\rho)\) is the diagonal density matrix \(\mathrm{diag}(p)\) with
\(p_s=|z_s|^2/\|z\|^2\) — *exactly* the Born closure. Petz monotonicity of
the Bures metric under \(\mathcal P\) (the H-theorem half of Cor 9.9) is
the standard data-processing inequality at SLD/Bures (Paper 18 §6); T7
names this as "decoherence = closure of phase" and "quantization = the
cover."

### 2.5.2 The 3D answer is geometric, not just selection-theoretic (T1)

At \(N=4\) (the tetrahedral simplex), \(H_0\) has dimension 3 and T1 §6
constructs an *intrinsic vector algebra* on \(H_0\) — inner product, cross
product \(\times_S\) defined via Hodge duality on \(\mathbb R^4\), Rodrigues
rotation \(R_{n,\theta}\) — that *reproduces ordinary 3-dimensional Euclidean
vector calculus exactly* in the Helmert frame (T1 §7.3, with explicit
verification of \(e_1'\times_S e_2'=e_3'\) and cyclic identities). The
isomorphism is not analogical: T1 proves the simplicial algebra on \(H_0\)
*is* ordinary 3D vector algebra at \(N=4\).

**Consequence for §8.** Paper 32 §8 reaches "spatial dimension 3" by two
independent routes: Theorem 8.2 (Hodge / \(\mathfrak{so}(3)\) /
\(\Lambda^2(\mathbb R^n)\cong\mathbb R^n\) only at \(n=3\)), and Theorem 8.3
(the conductor count \(1+2=3\) of the closure-valid block on \(\mathbb
Z/6\)). T1 supplies a *third*, direct route: the intrinsic 3D Euclidean
vector algebra *is* the simplicial geometry at \(N=4\), with no need for
selection or projection at all. The three routes confirm each other and pin
Corollary 8.4 ("experiencing space as three-dimensional is the closure
certificate of having mass") to a known geometric anchor: at \(N=4\), the
simplex's tangent space *is* ordinary 3-space, period.

The closure-valid block \(A_2\oplus A_3\) of dimension 3 on the \(\mathbb
Z/6\) row of Paper 32 §6 is *not* the \(N=4\) tetrahedral case directly —
\(H_6\) has dimension 5, and \(A_2\oplus A_3\) is a 3-dimensional sub-bundle
inside it — but the *dimension-3-as-natural-3-space* fact is the same. The
inertial projector \(\Pi_\tau\) extracts a 3D sub-bundle from \(H_6\) that
inherits the same internal 3D vector algebra T1 establishes at \(N=4\).

### 2.5.3 Radical arithmetic complements conductor arithmetic (T4, T9, T10)

Paper 32's central computation works entirely in the *conductor arithmetic*
of \(\mathbb Z/6\) — the divisor lattice \(\{1,2,3,6\}\) and the conductor
packets \(H_d\) for \(d\mid 6\), \(d>1\). T4 introduces a *complementary*
arithmetic axis: the **multiquadratic radical clock**
\[
K_N=\mathbb Q(\sqrt p:p\le N,\;p\text{ prime}),
\]
generated by the Helmert unit normalizers \(\sqrt{k(k+1)}\) along the
dimensional ladder. For \(N=6\),
\[
K_6=\mathbb Q(\sqrt 2,\sqrt 3,\sqrt 5),\qquad[K_6:\mathbb Q]=8,
\]
*the radical \(\sqrt 5\) enters even though \(5\nmid 6\)*, because the
dimensional ladder traverses primes up to \(N\) regardless of which primes
divide \(N\). T4 Theorem 5.1 / T0 Theorem 5.1 proves that \(K_N\) grows
properly over \(K_{N-1}\) iff \(N\) is prime — the *prime-rung* criterion of
the radical clock.

T10 places this in the gauge corridor: \(K_N^{(m)}=\mathbb
Q(\zeta_m,\sqrt[m]p:p\le N)\) at gauge rung \(m\). For the cubic rung
\(m=3\) — the gauge rung relevant to Paper 32's Amari–Chentsov tensor —
\[
K_6^{(3)}=\mathbb Q(\zeta_3,\sqrt[3]2,\sqrt[3]3,\sqrt[3]5)
\]
sits at degree \(2\cdot 3^3=54\) over \(\mathbb Q\) (T10 §5.1). The cubic
selection rule \(k+l+m\equiv 0\pmod 6\) (Lemma 6.1a) and the multiquadratic
radical clock \(K_6\) are two faces of the *same* arithmetic substrate of
\(\mathbb Z/6\) — one a Pontryagin-dual identity (T3), one a Kummer-tower
identity (T4/T10).

**T9's transfer matrix.** T9 establishes the explicit
\((N-1)\times(N-1)\) unitary transfer matrix \(U_{kc}=\langle
e_k,\tilde\chi_c\rangle\) between the Helmert (radical) basis and the
character (cyclotomic) basis of \(H_N\) — *the apex's arithmetic Jacobian*.
The entries factor as
\[
U_{kc}=R_k\cdot C_{kc},\qquad R_k\in K_N,\;C_{kc}\in\mathbb Q(\zeta_N),
\]
with the radical \(R_k=1/\sqrt{Nk(k+1)}\) depending only on the Helmert
axis \(k\) and the cyclotomic polynomial
\(C_{kc}=(1-(k+1)\omega^{kc}+k\omega^{(k+1)c})/(1-\omega^c)\) depending only
on the character axis \(c\) (T9 Cor 2.4). For \(N=6\), \(U_6\) is a
\(5\times 5\) matrix over \(K_6\cdot\mathbb Q(\zeta_6)=\mathbb Q(\sqrt
2,\sqrt 3,\sqrt 5,\zeta_6)\) of degree 16 (T9 §5.2, anchored to T0 §5.5).

**Consequence for §6.** Paper 32 §6's character-basis computation has a
complementary *Helmert-basis* form, related by the explicit transfer matrix
\(U_6\). The cubic selection rule \(T_{klm}\ne 0\iff k+l+m\equiv 0\pmod 6\)
on the character basis transforms via \(U_6\) into a *triangular
Helmert-basis selection rule* governed by triangle-incidence data of the
integer Helmert contrasts \(h_k=(1,\ldots,1,-k,0,\ldots,0)\); the
cross-prime forcing of Lemma 6.1 / Corollary 6.1d that "\(A_2\) cannot
participate cubically except through \(M\)" reads, in Helmert basis, as a
*block structure on the cubic contraction \(T(h_a,h_b,h_c)\) sorted by
max-index* (T9 Theorem 7.3, the cubic Helmert selection rule). T9 §5.2 / T0
§5.5 exhibits the \(N=6\) Helmert-basis cubic as a specific arithmetic
object, complementing the character-basis enumeration of Paper 32 §6.

This is *not* a new technical result — both bases compute the same finite
tensor — but a *complete dual-basis check* of Paper 32's central
computation, recorded so the cubic vertex selection rule is anchored
simultaneously in the cyclotomic (T3 / Paper 32 §6) *and* the radical (T4
/ T9) arithmetic substrates of \(H_6\).

### 2.5.4 The conductor-6 carry packet \(M\) is the multiplicative-unit subgroup \((\mathbb Z/6)^\times\): the additive–multiplicative coincidence at primorial \(\mathbb Z/6\)

This is the most striking new arithmetic identification the T-series
substrate provides for Paper 32. T8 names the multiplicative-character dual
of \(\mathbb Z/N\): the *Dirichlet characters* of \((\mathbb Z/N)^\times\),
the *multiplicative Pontryagin dual* of the unit group. At \(N=6\),
\[
(\mathbb Z/6)^\times=\{1,5\},\qquad\varphi(6)=2,
\]
the set of residues coprime to 6, of cardinality 2.

The *additive* characters of conductor \(N=6\) (i.e. the characters
\(\chi_c(s)=e^{2\pi ics/6}\) of full additive conductor) are exactly those
with \(\gcd(c,6)=1\) — equivalently \(c\in\{1,5\}\). So the conductor-6
carry packet of Paper 32 §6,
\[
M=\{\chi_1,\chi_5\}\subset\widehat{\mathbb Z/6},
\]
is *index-identical* — as a subset of \(\mathbb Z/6\) — to the
multiplicative-unit subgroup \((\mathbb Z/6)^\times\subset\mathbb Z/6\). The
two-element set \(\{1,5\}\) carries both meanings simultaneously: as
additive-character indices it is the conductor-6 packet; as residues it is
the multiplicative-unit subgroup.

The complementary identification is equally clean:

- \(A_2=\{\chi_3\}\) corresponds to \(c=3\), the unique non-unit with
  \(\gcd(3,6)=3\) (a non-unit because \(3\mid 6\)) — the "single-prime axis
  factoring through \(\mathbb Z/2\)".
- \(A_3=\{\chi_2,\chi_4\}\) corresponds to \(c\in\{2,4\}\), the two non-units
  with \(\gcd(c,6)=2\) — the "single-prime axis factoring through \(\mathbb
  Z/3\)".

So as a *set* (modulo the gauge mode \(c=0\)):
\[
\widehat{\mathbb Z/6}\setminus\{0\}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\{2,3,4\}}_{\text{non-units of }\mathbb Z/6}
\;\sqcup\;
\underbrace{\{1,5\}}_{(\mathbb Z/6)^\times}.
\]
Re-reading the conductor decomposition of \(H_6\):
\[
\boxed{
H_6
\;=\;
\underbrace{A_2\oplus A_3}_{\text{non-unit additive subspace}}
\;\oplus\;
\underbrace{M}_{\text{unit additive subspace }(\mathbb Z/6)^\times}.
}
\]

**Observation 2.5.4 (the unit-residual identification). [P]** *The
closure-valid block of the time-translation projection \(\Pi_\tau\) on
\(\mathbb Z/6\) (Theorem 6.2) is the additive subspace spanned by characters
indexed by **non-units** of \(\mathbb Z/6\); the gravity-adjacent residual
is the additive subspace spanned by characters indexed by **multiplicative
units** of \(\mathbb Z/6\). Gravity localizes to the multiplicative-unit
subgroup of the conductor row.*

**Proof.** Combine §6.1's conductor decomposition with the elementary
arithmetic identification
\(\mathrm{cond}(\chi_c)=6\iff\gcd(c,6)=1\iff c\in(\mathbb Z/6)^\times\),
and
\(\mathrm{cond}(\chi_c)<6\iff\gcd(c,6)>1\iff c\not\in(\mathbb Z/6)^\times\).
The latter is the standard cyclotomic identification
\(\mathrm{cond}(\chi_c)=N/\gcd(c,N)\) (T3 §3 / T0 §4.2). \(\blacksquare\)

**Consistency at the cubic vertex (the L2 cross-prime forcing has a
unit-arithmetic reading).** Lemma 6.2(L2) of Paper 32 §6.2A says every
\(A_2\)-leg closing triple \((3,\ell,m)\) requires \(\ell+m\equiv 3\pmod
6\), and the exhaustive enumeration on \(\{1,\ldots,5\}\) gives
\(\{1,2\},\{4,5\}\) — both pairs carry one unit \((1\) or \(5)\) and one
non-unit \((2\) or \(4)\). In unit-arithmetic language: *every cubic
closing triple involving the non-unit 3 carries exactly one unit*.
Equivalently: a cubic vertex cannot be supported on the non-unit subspace
alone (Lemma 6.2(L1)'s pure-\(A_2\) vacuity is the case "three non-units
\(3+3+3=9\) fails to close"). The cross-prime forcing of Cor 6.1d.2 reads,
in unit-arithmetic terms: *every non-unit-only cubic closing triple fails
to close; every closing triple involving the non-unit 3 has exactly one
unit leg; the remaining closing triples couple multiple units*.

The *units carry the cubic charge*; the non-units are cubic-isolated unless
they couple through units; the closing triples \(\{2,2,2\}\) and
\(\{4,4,4\}\) (the surviving pure-non-unit closures in \(A_3\)) are exactly
the triples of non-units all coming from the *same* maximal proper divisor
(\(\gcd=2\), factoring through \(\mathbb Z/3\)). The closure-valid block
\(A_2\oplus A_3\) of Theorem 6.2 is exactly the non-unit subspace; the
part annihilated by \(\Pi_\tau\) — the carry/residual subspace \(M\) — is
exactly the unit subspace. Gravity localizes to units.

**Consequence for the four-thread arithmetic root (§6.2A Cor 6.3).** All
four threads of Cor 6.3 (T1: Paper 20 OQ1 ratio; T2: color protection of
the chirality bit; T3: Friston gradient/least-action fork; T4: Paper 12
preconditioner blind spot) read identically through unit arithmetic:

| Thread | Cor 6.3 reading | Unit-arithmetic reading |
|---|---|---|
| **T1** (Paper 20 OQ1) | Pure-\(A_2\) cubic self-energy = 0; no simplex-mass ratio | Pure-non-unit cubic vacuity (no closing triple of three non-units of common type) |
| **T2** (Paper 16 / 33 chirality) | \(A_2\)–\(A_3\) channel forced through \(M\) | Non-unit \(A_2\)–non-unit \(A_3\) channel forced through *units* |
| **T3** (Friston FEP-3) | \(F=F_V+F_M\); \(V\)-supported reversible, \(M\)-supported gradient-of-entropy | Non-unit support is reversible; unit support is the gradient direction = arrow of time |
| **T4** (Paper 12 / beyond_smooth) | 18/20 cross-packet content invisible to second-order Fisher | 18/20 *involves units*; second-order Fisher sees only the non-unit block |

Four threads, one congruence, one additive–multiplicative coincidence at
primorial \(\mathbb Z/6\). The unit-arithmetic reading does not change any
tier; it provides a fifth structural recurrence of the same root (next to
the four of Cor 6.3) and connects Paper 32's central computation to T8's
multiplicative-character / Dirichlet-\(L\)-function program (T8 §5 finite
Dirichlet polynomials; T8 §7.3 RH-shaped questions on the multiplicative
apex). Cf. ledger items DD (FEP-3 settlement), FF (T-series anchor), and
GG (this identification).

**Extension to primorial rows.** The additive–multiplicative coincidence
extends naturally to every squarefree primorial \(\mathbb Z/P_k=\mathbb
Z/(p_1\cdots p_k)\): the conductor-\(P_k\) packet (the full-conductor
carry, where the gravity-adjacent residual lives at the cubic-vertex level
of the higher row) has real dimension \(\varphi(P_k)\) and is generated by
characters indexed by \((\mathbb Z/P_k)^\times\); the single-prime axes
\(A_{p_i}\) and proper-divisor packets fill the non-unit complement. So:

\[
\boxed{
\text{at every squarefree primorial row, gravity localizes to the unit
subgroup }(\mathbb Z/P_k)^\times.
}
\]

For \(\mathbb Z/30=\mathbb Z/(2\cdot 3\cdot 5)\) (Paper 21 / Paper 22's
next primorial row), \((\mathbb Z/30)^\times=\{1,7,11,13,17,19,23,29\}\)
has \(\varphi(30)=8\) elements; the conductor-30 packet \(M_{30}\) has
real dimension 8, and the closure-valid non-unit block has real dimension
\((30-1)-8=21\) — a structural prediction for the Paper 21 / Paper 22
audit of the \(P_3\) row. This is recorded as **ledger item GG** in §12;
not asserted as a new theorem, but as the structural form Paper 32's
analysis takes on the next primorial row.

**Connection to Paper 22 (RH).** The all-order Weil positivity of Paper 22
is, in T8 §5 / §7.3 language, the conjectural positivity of finite
Dirichlet polynomials \(L_x(\beta,\psi)\) attached to the
categorical-belief simplex in the all-order limit. The cubic-order match
of §7 Theorem 7.1 is the \(P_2\)-row, order-3 specialization. The
unit-arithmetic identification of this section gives the bridge a
*Gauss-sum-flavour* — the natural intertwiner of multiplicative and
additive characters of \(\mathbb Z/N\) (T8 §4) — and strengthens the §7
RH-bridge status pin: the all-order limit of \(\mathcal Q_n(\mathscr R_n
f)\to\mathcal W_\zeta(f*f^*)\) (Paper 22 Conj 5.2) lives most naturally
on the *multiplicative apex* of T8 §2, with the finite-Dirichlet-polynomial
structure of T8 §5 supplying its bridge to the classical \(L\)-functions
of analytic number theory. *No new theorem*; the substrate is rewritten
in standard Gauss-sum language.

### 2.5.5 Note on the gauge corridor (T0 / T10)

T0 / T10 places \(\Phi=\log Z\) (Paper 32 §3) inside a continuous *gauge
corridor* \(\{M_r:r\in\mathbb R\}\) of gauge choices. \(\Phi\) is the
log-of-arithmetic-mean (\(r=1\)) rung of this corridor; T5 worked the
harmonic (\(r=-1\)) polar partner; T6 worked the RMS / Born (\(r=2\), the
"amplitude is primal" rung) gauge that underlies the square-root sphere of
T1. The Amari–Chentsov cubic tensor lives at \(r=3\) (T10 §5.1). Each gauge
rung carries its own Kummer clock \(K_N^{(m)}\) (T10 §4) and its own
character-twisting selection rule (T10 §2).

This positions Paper 32's framework: the *one functional* \(\Phi\) of the
title is the gauge-1 rung of a multi-rung gauge corridor; the *four
derivatives* of the subtitle are the order-0,1,2,3 cumulants on that rung
(T2). The structural unification of Paper 32 (one functional, four
derivatives) is preserved in the T-substrate as a uniform statement at
every rung (T2 / T10): at each gauge \(r\), the same four-derivative tower
generates position, metric, cubic vertex, and closure defect, with the
gauge choice controlling *which physical interpretation* each layer carries
(mass for \(r=1\); amplitude for \(r=2\); reciprocal for \(r=-1\)). Paper
32's space=mass=second-cumulant identity (§4 Theorem 4.1) is the \(r=1\)
specialization; T6 §3 contains the \(r=2\) (Born/RMS) statement and T5 §6
the \(r=-1\) (harmonic) statement.

This is recorded as a *framing*: it does not change any of Paper 32's
tiered claims, but it places them within the gauge corridor and forecasts
that the "polar dual" of Paper 32 (the harmonic version, with gravity
localized through the reciprocal closure of T5) is a natural sibling
investigation, not a separate framework.

### 2.5.6 The Apex Vertex Calculus (T11): Paper 32 §6 as a worked example of a general theorem

T11 (*The Apex Vertex Calculus*, Murillo Montero 2026) elevates Paper 32's
\(\mathbb Z/6\) central computation from a row-specific calculation to a
**worked example of a general theorem** about conductor-fusion boundaries.
This subsection records the load-bearing identifications.

**T11's three structure theorems.**

*T11 Theorem 2.1 (Apex Feynman Rule).* For every \(n\ge 0\) and \(c\in
\mathbb Z/N\):
\[
\partial_\beta^n\mathcal M_x(0,c)
=
\sum_{\substack{a_1,\ldots,a_n\in\mathbb Z/N\\a_1+\cdots+a_n\equiv c\,(\bmod\,N)}}
\widehat y_{a_1}\cdots\widehat y_{a_n}.
\]
The \(n\)-leg apex vertex is
\(V_n(a_1,\ldots,a_n;c)=\mathbf 1[a_1+\cdots+a_n\equiv c\pmod N]\) — *finite
character-conservation rule for all orders*.

*T11 Theorem 3.1 (Conductor Fusion Theorem).* Packet products
\(H_{d_1}\star\cdots\star H_{d_n}\) decompose prime-locally by \(p\)-adic
cancellation rules: with \(B_p=\max_i v_p(d_i)\), \(m_p=\#\{i:v_p(d_i)=B_p\}\),
the output exponent \(\gamma_p\) is determined by:
- \(B_p=0\) ⇒ \(\gamma_p=0\)
- odd \(p, m_p=1\) ⇒ \(\gamma_p=B_p\)
- odd \(p, m_p\ge 2\) ⇒ \(\gamma_p\in\{0,1,\ldots,B_p\}\) (cancellation allowed)
- \(p=2, m_p\) odd ⇒ \(\gamma_p=B_p\) (NO cancellation at prime 2 with odd multiplicity)
- \(p=2, m_p\) even ⇒ \(\gamma_p\in\{0,\ldots,B_p-1\}\) (cancellation allowed at prime 2 with even multiplicity)

*T11 Theorem 4.1 (Projection-Closure Theorem).* For \(H_S=\bigoplus_{d\in S}H_d\),
the projected \(n\)-vertex closes on \(H_S\) iff \(S\) is closed under
\(n\)-fold conductor fusion. The universal leakage support is the
**conductor-fusion boundary** \(\partial_n S=\bigcup_{d_i\in S}\mathsf{Fuse}(d_1,\ldots,d_n)\setminus(S\cup\{1\})\).

**Paper 32 §6 results identified as special cases.**

T11 specializes back to Paper 32 §6's central theorems exactly. Four
identifications:

| Paper 32 result | T11 theorem | Specialization |
|---|---|---|
| Lemma 6.1a: \(T_{klm}\ne 0\iff k+l+m\equiv 0\,(\bmod\,6)\) | T11 Thm 2.1 | \(n=3, N=6, c=0\) case of the Apex Feynman Rule |
| Cor 6.1d.1: \(A_3\) self-closure at \(\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}\) | T11 Thm 3.1 | odd-prime \(p=3, m=3\), \(m\ge 2\) ⇒ cancellation allowed; output reaches conductor 1 |
| Cor 6.1d.2: pure-\(A_2\) cubic vacuity | T11 Thm 3.1 | \(p=2, m=3\) odd ⇒ NO cancellation at prime 2; output stays at conductor 2; cannot close to conductor 1 |
