### 2.5.6b The Finite Apex Determinacy Theorem (T12): the order/tensor axis closed

T12 (*The Finite Apex Determinacy Theorem*, Murillo Montero 2026) discharges
RR forward door 8 (Newton-Hankel finite-rank closure) and closes the
order/tensor axis of Paper 32's framework. The key result:

\[
\boxed{\text{apex tower of } x \;\;\cong\;\; \mathbb R[t]/(P_x)}
\]

where \(P_x(t) = \prod_{j=1}^r (t - \lambda_j)\) and \(\lambda_1, \ldots,
\lambda_r\) are the **distinct values of the centered ledger \(y_s\)**.
The apex tower — moments, cumulants, channel coefficients, the apex
generating function — is the *finite quotient algebra* \(\mathbb R[t]/(P_x)\)
seen through scalar and channel linear functionals.

**T12's six theorems (briefly).**

1. **Finite Spectral Support (T12 Thm 1):** \(\mu_n = \sum_{j=1}^r W_j \lambda_j^n\). Scalar moments depend only on distinct ledger levels and aggregate weights.
2. **Hankel Rank Closure (T12 Thm 2):** \(\operatorname{rank}H_k = \min(k+1, r)\). Moments obey the recurrence \(\mu_{n+r} + a_{r-1}\mu_{n+r-1} + \cdots + a_0 \mu_n = 0\) where \(P_x(t) = t^r + a_{r-1}t^{r-1} + \cdots + a_0\).
3. **Newton-Hankel Reconstruction (T12 Thm 3):** The finite prefix \(\mu_0, \ldots, \mu_{2r-1}\) uniquely determines \(P_x\), the levels, and the weights.
4. **Channel-Resolved Closure (T12 Thm 4):** Every Fourier channel \(M_c^{(n)} = \sum_s \alpha_c(s) y_s^n\) obeys the SAME closure polynomial \(P_x\).
5. **Cumulant Closure (T12 Thm 5):** All cumulants are generated by the same finite spectral data \(\{(\lambda_j, W_j)\}\).
6. **Finite Apex Determinacy (T12 Thm 6):** The apex tower \(\cong \mathbb R[t]/(P_x)\) as a quotient algebra.

**Critical implications for Paper 32.**

| Paper 32 content | T12 reading |
|---|---|
| §3 "infinite cumulant tower" | **Finite quotient algebra \(\mathbb R[t]/(P_x)\)** |
| §10.6.7m connected quartic \(\kappa_4 = \mu_4 - 3\mu_2^2\) | finitely determined by \(r\) spectral atoms |
| Each character channel \(\mathcal M_x(\beta, c)\) | obeys SAME degree-\(r\) recurrence (T12 Thm 4) |
| §10.6.7k universal all-order leakage support | order-axis finiteness ⇒ at most \(r\) independent scalar orders |
| Paper 22 RH all-order limit | **finitely many cumulants suffice** (T12 Thm 5) |
| Paper 47 audit δ V ⊥ M decoupling all-orders | extends by finite-rank argument |
| RR forward door 8 (Newton-Hankel finite-rank) | **DISCHARGED** by T12 Thm 3 |

**Paper 32 §3 "categorical effective action" reading.**

Paper 32 §3 (Claim 3.1) treats \(\Phi = \log Z\) as the generator of an
"infinite tower" of cumulants — position, metric, cubic vertex, quartic
closure-defect, and beyond. T12 makes this structurally precise:

\[
\Phi = \log Z = K(\beta)\big|_{\beta=1}, \quad
\kappa_n = K^{(n)}(0)\text{ determined by } \mathbb R[t]/(P_x).
\]

The cumulant tower is *finite-rank in scalar projection*. Higher cumulants
continue forever as numbers but are **not algebraically free** — they are
polynomial functions of the first \(r\) moments via the Hankel recurrence
plus Möbius inversion (T12 §6).

**Slogan (T12-style):**

> *Higher order is real, but not freely new.*

**Specific consequences at Z/6.**

- **Uniform centroid \(p_* = 1/6\):** all \(\log x_s = -\log 6\), so \(y_s = 0\) for all \(s\) ⇒ \(r = 1\), \(P_x(t) = t\), scalar apex tower trivial: \(\mathbb R[t]/(t) = \mathbb R\). Paper 32's multivariate cubic structure constant \(T_{klm} = 216\) lives in the *multivariate* framework (T12 §11.3 forward door), not the scalar moment framework T12 directly addresses.
- **Generic point in \(\Delta^5\):** all \(p_s\) distinct, so \(r = 6\), \(P_x\) is degree 6, scalar apex tower has *at most 6 independent moments*.
- **Symmetric 2+2+2 point (T12 §8 worked example):** \(r = 3\), \(P_x(t) = (t-a)(t-b)(t-c)\), \(\mu_{n+3} = -(ab+ac+bc)\mu_{n+1} + abc\,\mu_n\) (using \(\mu_1 = 0\) for centered ledger).
- **Phase boundaries:** the discriminant \(\Delta(P_x) = \prod_{i<j}(\lambda_i - \lambda_j)^2\) marks where ledger levels collide — natural phase-boundary geometry in the simplex (T12 §11.2 forward door).

**Strengthening of Paper 22's RH-bridge program.**

Paper 22's all-order Noether-Weil positivity (cubic-order matched in Paper 32 §7) is a statement about an infinite sequence of cumulants. T12 Thm 5 implies **finitely many cumulants suffice** — the all-order limit is determined by finite spectral data. This makes the all-order RH-bridge a *finite-data* statement at each row, removing the "infinite tower" framing Paper 22 implicitly uses.

**Strengthening of Paper 47's audit δ.**

Paper 47 audit δ proves V ⊥ M decoupling at all orders. T12 implies: this needs to be checked only up to order \(r\) (the Hankel rank of the relevant ledger), and the rest follow by recurrence. Audit δ's "all-orders machine-floor verification" at orders \(m = 2..6\) on \(\mathbb Z/\{6, 30, 210\}\) is now structurally complete — orders \(m > 6\) at \(\mathbb Z/6\) are *forced* by the degree-6 recurrence.

**What T12 does NOT do (limitations from §10).**

- **Scalar only.** T12 treats one scalar ledger. Multivariate tensor framework (Paper 32 §6's full \(H_6\)-tensor structure) requires T12 §11.3 forward door (\(\mathbb R[t_1, \ldots, t_m]/I\)).
- **Doesn't replace T11.** T11 closes the character/conductor axis; T12 closes the order/tensor axis. Both are needed.
- **Numerical stability separate** (T12 §10.4): exact algebra valid, but ledger-level near-collisions create conditioning issues.
- **Commutative only** — noncommutative apex (Paper 18 Bures lift) is T12 §11.5 forward door.

**Combined T11 + T12 view: the framework is totally finite arithmetic perturbation theory.**

| Axis | Tool | Closure |
|---|---|---|
| Character/conductor axis | T11 vertex calculus | conductor fusion \(\mathsf{Fuse}_n\) |
| Order/tensor axis | T12 finite determinacy | Hankel recurrence \(P_x\) |
| Combined | T11 + T12 | finite Feynman rules with finite recurrence |

**The categorical-simplex framework has, with T11 + T12, no algebraically
free degrees of freedom beyond a finite combinatorial structure.** Paper 32's
"depth of closure" and "all-order V ⊥ M" claims are now finite-data
statements throughout.

**Tier.** [P, structural — T12 substrate]. Discharges RR forward door 8.
No tier upgrades on Paper 32 results; the discharge is at the level of
*why* the framework's tower is closed (finite quotient algebra) rather
than *whether* the §6 / §10.6.7 results hold.

Recorded as **ledger item TT** in §12, anchoring T12 alongside T11.

### 2.5.6c Exact Simplicial Renormalization (T13): the projection-residual axis closed

**T13** (Murillo Montero 2026, "Exact Simplicial Renormalization — Integrating Out Conductor Boundaries in the Finite Apex Algebra") proves nine structure theorems that turn T11's "projection has a boundary source" and T12's "induced order tower is finite" into a finite Wilsonian renormalization theory. **T13 is the closure of the third structural axis of the categorical-simplex framework: the projection / RG-flow axis.** Together, T11 (character/conductor axis), T12 (order/tensor axis), and T13 (projection/RG axis) leave the framework as **a finite arithmetic perturbation theory with three closed structural dimensions**.

**T13's nine theorems (compressed statement form):**

1. **Boundary Source Decomposition (T13 Thm 1).** Every \(n\)-leg apex interaction with inputs in a retained sector \(H_S=\bigoplus_{d\in S}H_d\) decomposes uniquely as
   \[
   V_n=\Pi_SV_n+(1-\Pi_S)V_n,
   \qquad
   (1-\Pi_S)V_n(H_S^{\otimes n})\subseteq\bigoplus_{d\in\partial_nS}H_d
   \]
   modulo the trivial constant packet \(H_1\). The boundary support \(\partial_nS=\operatorname{Fuse}_n(S)\setminus(S\cup\{1\})\) is sharp.

2. **Exact Boundary Integration (T13 Thm 2).** Eliminating the boundary source is exact finite marginalization plus logarithmic connected projection: \(K_{\mathrm{eff},S}(J_S)=K(J_S,0)\), and the retained \(n\)-point cumulants are exactly the full connected cumulants with all external legs in \(S\). **No approximation.**

3. **Schur Complement Renormalization (T13 Thm 3).** At quadratic order, the effective retained kernel after integrating out the boundary is exactly the Schur complement \(Q^{\mathrm{eff}}_S=Q_{SS}-Q_{SB}Q_{BB}^{-1}Q_{BS}\).

4. **Finite Renormalization Closure (T13 Thm 4).** Every retained effective cumulant tower lives inside a finite coordinate algebra \(A_S=\mathbb R[z_1,\ldots,z_m]/I(Y_S(\Omega))\); each diagonal source direction is governed by a finite closure polynomial \(P_{S,h}\) (the projected analog of T12's \(P_x\)).

5. **Beta Function as Boundary Curvature (T13 Thm 5).** Running couplings between retained sectors \(S\subseteq S'\) are exactly the newly admitted boundary packets:
   \[
   \beta^{(n)}_{S\to S'}=0\iff\partial_nS\cap(S'\setminus S)=\varnothing.
   \]
   At maximal target, \(\beta^{(n)}=0\) ⇔ \(S\) is \(n\)-fusion-closed. **Couplings run exactly when projection crosses a conductor-fusion boundary.**

6. **Fixed Sector / Renormalizability (T13 Thm 6).** A retained sector \(H_S\) is **exactly renormalizable** under \(n\)-leg apex interactions if and only if \(S\) is \(n\)-fusion-closed. If \(S\) is not closed, the minimal exactly renormalizable completion is \(H_{\operatorname{Cl}_n(S)}\). **This is the criterion.**

7. **Universality of Fusion Completion (T13 Thm 7).** The completed sector \(H_{\operatorname{Cl}_n(S)}\) is independent of coordinates, basis, Helmert presentation, or character representatives. **The universal object is the packet completion, not the projection matrix.**

8. **Exact \(N=6\) Four-Sector Renormalization (T13 Thm 8).** On \(\mathbb Z/6\) with \(S=\{2,3\}\), the cubic conductor-fusion boundary is \(\partial_3\{2,3\}=\{6\}\). Therefore any cubic effective theory retaining \(A_2\oplus A_3\) but eliminating \(M=H_6\) has an exact forced boundary correction supported on \(M\). **This is Paper 32 Theorem 6.2 promoted to a renormalization-theoretic theorem.**

9. **Fusion-Closure Renormalization Group (T13 Thm 9, capstone).** The map \(S\mapsto\operatorname{Cl}_n(S)\) is an extensive, monotone, idempotent closure operator on the finite packet lattice \(\mathcal P(\mathcal D_N)\). Its fixed points are exactly the exactly renormalizable packet sectors. The discrete boundary-adjunction flow \(S_{k+1}=S_k\cup\partial_nS_k\) terminates after finitely many steps at \(\operatorname{Cl}_n(S_0)\). **Universality classes of the finite simplicial RG are the fibers of the closure operator.**

**Central compression (T13 Abstract):**
\[
\boxed{
\text{RG flow}=\text{boundary adjunction},
\qquad
\text{fixed points}=\text{fusion-closed sectors}.
}
\]

**Application to Paper 32.** T13 promotes Paper 32 §6 from a finite Fourier calculation to a finite renormalization theorem. Specifically:

- **§6 Theorem 6.2 (gravity-adjacent residual = \(M\) packet) IS the \(N=6, n=3, S=\{2,3\}\) instance of T13 Theorem 8** at projection-residual / RG-fixed-point level. The cubic boundary \(\partial_3\{2,3\}=\{6\}\) is the cubic *RG-forced* extension, not a row-specific structural choice. **Paper 32 v0.2.5 has not yet stated this finite-RG framing.**

- **§6.2 Lemma 6.1a (cubic selection rule \(a+b+c\equiv 0\pmod 6\))** is the additive-character form of T13's conductor-fusion mechanism at \(n=3\); the closing-triple count \(T_{klm}=216\) is the finite cardinality entering the boundary-source decomposition (T13 Thm 1).

- **§3 (categorical effective action)** is the cumulant generating functional whose sectors T13 projects; T13's Thm 4 says the resulting effective tower is finite-data, matching T12's apex closure polynomial when the retained observable is the original scalar ledger.

- **§7 Bures lift / Paper 22 RH-bridge** acquires a finite-RG reading: the §6 cubic-residual structure IS the cubic RG-flow fixed-point structure of \(\mathbb Z/6\); the squared-norm bridge (project_paperIII_squared_norm_bridge_open) reads as the boundary-integration identity at the squared-norm Gram form.

- **Paper 47 V/M decomposition at Z/30:** previously discharged by T11 + T12 as forced cubic-completion (§10.6.7l / item SS); T13 now adds the **renormalization-theoretic content**: Paper 47 V/M is the *unique RG-fixed-point sector* at Z/30 cubic, with V = retained, M = the cubic boundary source that must be either adjoined (giving an exactly renormalizable theory) or integrated out (producing the V-effective theory of Paper 47).

- **§10.6.7j fusion-closed sub-theories at Z/6 cubic** (5 sectors \(\emptyset, \{A_2\}, \{A_3\}, \{A_2,M\}, \{A_2,A_3,M\}\)) is now read as the **lattice of cubic RG-fixed points** on \(\mathbb Z/6\); the new sector \(\{A_2,M\}\) (parity + gravity-adjacent without color) is a *non-trivial intermediate RG-fixed point* in the closure lattice — the structural location of any "between-Z/6-and-fully-coupled" effective theory.

- **§10.6.7k universal all-order leakage support** \(\partial_n V=\{6\}\) for all \(n\ge 3\) at Z/6: this is now the statement that **M is the universal RG-boundary of V at all orders**; integrating out M produces exact effective V-couplings via T13 Thm 2 at every order.

**Three new structural slogans (T13-level):**

(S1) *A projected finite apex theory is **exactly renormalizable** iff its retained packet set is **fusion-closed**.* (T13 Thm 6) — the criterion for "is this projection a theory of its own?"

(S2) *In the finite simplicial setting, **couplings run exactly when the projection crosses a conductor-fusion boundary**.* (T13 Thm 5) — beta functions are not perturbative residuals; they are *new admitted packets*.

(S3) *Finite RG = the closure operator generated by conductor fusion.* (T13 Thm 9 capstone) — universality classes are *fibers of \(\operatorname{Cl}_n\)*, NOT trajectories in coupling space.

**Combined T11 + T12 + T13 picture (three closed axes).**

- **T11** closes the **character/conductor axis** (vertex calculus: which packets appear in \(V_n(H_S^{\otimes n})\)).
- **T12** closes the **order/tensor axis** (Hankel recurrence: how the moment/cumulant tower terminates).
- **T13** closes the **projection/RG axis** (closure operator: how omitted sectors pay back exactly via boundary integration).

The categorical-simplex framework, at primorial \(\mathbb Z/N\), has **no algebraically free degrees of freedom** beyond:

(i) the conductor lattice \(\mathcal D_N\) (a finite poset);

(ii) the packet decomposition \(H=\bigoplus_{d\in\mathcal D_N\cup\{1\}}H_d\) (orthogonal direct sum);

(iii) the closure operator \(\operatorname{Cl}_n\) (a finite combinatorial gadget);

(iv) the apex closure polynomial \(P_x\) of T12 (a univariate polynomial of degree \(r\le|\Omega|\)).

Paper 32's "depth of closure," "all-order V ⊥ M," and "gravity-as-projection-residual" claims are now finite-data, finite-RG statements throughout.

**What T13 does NOT claim (T13 §11, inherited by Paper 32):**

1. **Not continuum Wilsonian QFT.** T13 is finite, arithmetic, simplicial. Paper 32's PP item (Yang–Mills negative-bounding) is reinforced: T13 does NOT supply continuum RG, OS axioms, asymptotic freedom, Wilson-loop machinery, or spectral gap in GeV. **The finite RG of T13 is a separate object from the Wilsonian RG of constructive QFT** — sharing the *structural form* (boundary integration, closure-operator fixed points) but not the *continuum content*.

2. **Not all residuals are physical forces.** T13 proves projected apex residuals are *boundary sources*; physical interpretation (e.g., "this residual IS gravity") requires Paper 32's application map (§10.5 Unification Thesis), which remains [A, structural].

3. **Doesn't replace T12.** T13 says which packets must be included; T12 says why the induced order tower remains finite. Paper 32 needs both.

4. **Doesn't imply every projection should be closed.** Paper 32's gravity-adjacent residual (Theorem 6.2 / item GG) is an *omitted M*, not a closed-completed M; the framework deliberately retains a non-renormalizable projection (the "gravity-adjacent residual" carries non-closure exactly because M is excluded from the retained sector). T13 quantifies the *price* of this exclusion via Theorem 2 (exact boundary integration), not its avoidance.

**Tier.** [P, structural — T13 substrate]. No tier upgrades on Paper 32 §6 / §10.6 results; the discharge is at the level of *why* the framework's projection theory is exactly renormalization-theoretic at finite arithmetic, rather than *whether* the results hold.

Recorded as **ledger item UU** in §12, anchoring T13 alongside T11 and T12.

### 2.5.6d Projected Flatness and Curvature Linearization (T14): the geometry / curvature axis closed

**T14** (Murillo Montero 2026, "Projected Flatness and Curvature Linearization — Curvature as the Boundary Cost of Seeing a Flat Apex Algebra") proves six structure theorems culminating in a **5-way (6-way with beta) equivalence**: in the finite apex setting, *curvature*, *projection noncommutation*, *boundary leakage*, *renormalization source*, *Noether defect*, and *beta source* are **the same finite object** — the conductor-fusion boundary $\partial_n S$ — read in different languages.

**T14's six theorems (compressed):**

1. **Linear Lift (T14 Thm 1).** Nonlinear simplex dynamics is the normalized shadow of linear multiplication / transport in the ledger / apex algebra:
\[
p(\beta)=\mathcal N(q(\beta)),\quad q_s(\beta)=p_s(0)e^{\beta h_s},
\]
with the unnormalized lift $\dot q = M_h q$ linear. Equivalently, in centered log-ledger, motion is affine-linear. **The "lifting floor" method of the program is now a theorem, not a heuristic.**

2. **Curvature as Projection-Commutator (T14 Thm 2).** Let $E=M\times V$ carry a flat lifted connection $\nabla$ and let $\Pi$ be a smooth projection. The curvature of the projected connection $\nabla^\Pi=\Pi\nabla$ is
\[
R^\Pi(X,Y)=\Pi\big((\nabla_X\Pi)(\nabla_Y\Pi)-(\nabla_Y\Pi)(\nabla_X\Pi)\big)=\Pi\,d\Pi\wedge d\Pi\,\Pi.
\]
**Curvature is the obstruction to projection commuting with flat lifted transport.** Vanishes iff $[\nabla_X,\Pi]=0$.

3. **Boundary Curvature (T14 Thm 3).** For conductor-packet projections $\Pi_S$, the universal support of the projected curvature defect
\[
\mathcal R_S^{(n)}=(1-\Pi_S-\Pi_1)V_n\big|_{H_S^{\otimes n}}
\]
is **exactly the conductor-fusion boundary** $\partial_n S$, and this support is sharp. *The framework's "closure defect" language is literally curvature.*

4. **Schur Curvature Correction (T14 Thm 4).** Integrating out boundary packets renormalizes the retained curvature kernel by the Schur correction $Q^{\mathrm{eff}}_S=Q_{SS}-Q_{SB}Q_{BB}^{-1}Q_{BS}$; higher-order corrections are finite connected cumulant contractions with internal legs in $H_B$, external legs in $H_S$. *This is T13 Thm 3 read as a curvature correction.*

5. **Cubic Noether Defect (T14 Thm 5).** Inherited cubic conservation laws persist exactly on cubic-fusion-closed sectors. If $S$ is not cubic-fusion-closed, the Noether defect is supported on $\partial_3 S$. An individual conserved quantity may still persist exactly if its Noether covector annihilates the boundary leakage.

6. **Curvature-Renormalization-Noether Equivalence (T14 Thm 6, capstone).** For a retained sector $H_S$ and interaction order $n$, the following are equivalent:
   (i) $S$ is $n$-fusion-closed;
   (ii) the vertex closes on $H_S$ ($V_n(H_S^{\otimes n})\subseteq H_S\oplus H_1$);
   (iii) projected curvature defect $\mathcal R_S^{(n)}=0$;
   (iv) no boundary Schur / cumulant correction is universally forced;
   (v) every inherited $n$-order Noether identity persists without universal boundary defect;
   (vi) the discrete beta source vanishes for every enlargement.

   When the conditions fail, all five (six) failures are supported on the same boundary $\partial_n S$.

**Central compression (T14 Abstract):**
\[
\boxed{
\text{curvature}=\text{projection noncommutation}=\text{boundary leakage}=\text{renormalization source}=\text{Noether defect}=\text{beta source}.
}
\]

**Application to Paper 32 — the §3 / §5 promotion.** Paper 32 has used "closure defect" language since §3 and "gravity = projection-closure defect" since §5, at tier [A, structural] / [P, structural]. T14 makes this a *theorem of geometry*:

- **§3's "closure defect" of the categorical effective action = $\mathcal R_S^{(n)}$ of T14 Thm 3.**
- **§5's "cubic vertex = Amari–Chentsov; gravity = projection-closure defect" = T14 Thm 2 + Thm 3 at $n=3$, $N=6$, $S=\{2,3\}$, with curvature support $\partial_3\{2,3\}=\{6\}$.**
- **§6.4 Theorem 6.2's gravity-adjacent residual** is now four readings of one fact: (i) finite-Fourier boundary (T11), (ii) RG fixed-point obstruction (T13), (iii) Schur boundary source (V V / §10.6.7n), **(iv) projected curvature defect (T14 Thm 2 + Thm 3)** — all five identical at the channel level.

**Tier upgrade.** Paper 32 §3 / §5 / §6.4 curvature reading: **[A, structural] / [P, structural] → [P, proved geometric]** via T14 Thm 2 + Thm 3 + Thm 6 (Equivalence).

**Application to Item VV (Schur calculation at $\mathbb Z/6$, §10.6.7n).** The trivial quadratic Schur is now also "no quadratic curvature renormalization at uniform centroid." The two M-leakage channels $\{3,2,2\}\to\chi_1$ and $\{3,4,4\}\to\chi_5$ are *also* the explicit basis of the projected curvature defect $\mathcal R_V^{(3)}$ on $\mathbb Z/6$. Same channels, **five readings** (kinematic / RG-quadratic / channel-cubic / multiplicative-arithmetic / projected-curvature), all identical.

**Application to Item WW (Firewall Theorem).** T14 strengthens the Firewall Theorem with a fourth structural consequence (C4 below in §11.2.2): even **curvature** — naively thought to carry dimensionful content ($G$, Planck curvature, Ricci scalars in $\mathrm{km}^{-2}$) — lives on the finite combinatorial lattice $\mathcal D_N$ by T14 Thm 3. Curvature is internal to $\operatorname{Cl}_n$; dimensionful curvature scales remain external.

**Application to Item PP (Yang–Mills NEGATIVE assessment).** T14 §10.1 names *continuum curvature from finite projected flatness* as the **T15 problem** (named, not built). Item PP's "continuum-limit bridge" status changes from *absent* to *named-but-unbuilt*. Negative assessment preserved; structural shape acquires a named next step.

**Application to "lifting floor" methodology.** T14 Thm 1 (Linear Lift) is the theorem behind every "lift to apex, work linearly, project back" calculation the program has done since T0. The method is now grounded.

**Four closed structural axes (T11 + T12 + T13 + T14 picture).**

| Axis | Closed by | Object | Slogan |
|---|---|---|---|
| Character / conductor | T11 | Apex vertex calculus, $V_n$ | every projected simplex theory leaks through its conductor-fusion boundary |
| Order / tensor | T12 | Hankel recurrence, $P_x$ | higher order is real, but not freely new |
| Projection / RG | T13 | Closure operator $\operatorname{Cl}_n$ | finite RG = closure operator generated by conductor fusion |
| **Geometry / curvature** | **T14** | **Projection-commutator $R^\Pi$** | **curvature is what flat closure looks like after a nonclosed projection** |

Each axis identifies *the same finite object* $\partial_n S$ in a different language. **One boundary, four axes, six readings.**

**Three new structural slogans (T14-level):**

(S4) *Projected flatness ⇔ retained sector is fusion-closed.* (T14 capstone.)

(S5) *Curvature is the boundary cost of seeing a flat apex algebra through a nonclosed projection.* (T14 §1.)

(S6) *The geometry of a retained model is the cost of the projection by which it is seen.* (T14 §11 final compression.)

**What T14 does NOT claim (T14 §9, inherited by Paper 32):**

1. **Not all curvature in all mathematics is projection nonclosure.** Finite apex / simplicial mechanism only; template for continuum, not automatic theorem.

2. **Does not remove intrinsic geometry.** Explains how intrinsic curvature can arise from extrinsic flat lift plus nonclosed projection.

3. **Does not make boundary defects numerically large.** Support and mechanism are theorems; magnitude depends on coefficients and weights.

4. **Does not say every Noether law fails when $S$ is nonclosed.** Some conserved quantities can annihilate the boundary.

5. **Does not replace T13.** T13 gives RG closure operator; T14 explains why the same boundary is also curvature and Noether defect.

6. **Does not yet pass to the continuum.** That is the T15 problem (T14 §10.1).

**Tier.** [P, structural — T14 substrate; [P, proved geometric] for §3 / §5 promotion]. T14 promotes Paper 32 §3's "closure defect" from structural language to a *geometric theorem*; ledger items VV and WW acquire curvature readings.

Recorded as **ledger items XX** (T14 substrate + 4-axis closure) and **YY** (Paper 32 §3 / §5 / §6.4 tier upgrade to [P, proved geometric]) in §12.

