## 3. The One Functional

Let \(R\) be a finite modulus, \(Q=|R|\), \(\Delta_R^\circ\) the open
categorical simplex, \(H_R=\{u:\sum u_a=0\}\) its tangent hyperplane, and
\(\Phi(\theta)=\log Z(\theta)\) the log-partition of the categorical
exponential family in \(e\)-affine coordinates. Information geometry gives the
cumulant tower as the covariant derivative ladder of \(\Phi\):

\[
\nabla^{(0)}\Phi=\text{mean }\;\theta\text{-dual centroid},\qquad
\nabla^{(2)}\Phi=g\;(\text{Fisher}),\qquad
\nabla^{(3)}\Phi \sim T=K^{CH},\qquad
\nabla^{(e)}T=T^{(4)}.
\]

**Claim 3.1 (Categorical effective action). [P]**
\(\Phi\) plays the role a physical effective action plays in field theory: a
single convex functional whose derivative with respect to the state is the
source structure (here the metric), whose stationarity is the field equation
(here the cubic Noether/force law), and whose time-translation symmetry is the
conserved energy. Physics splits these into separately named objects —
position, mass, force, gravity — because it presupposes a manifold (spacetime)
prior to the matter on it. The categorical program has no prior manifold; the
manifold *is* the second cumulant. The four objects therefore remain four
derivatives of one functional, and the relations physics must *posit* (mass
sources curvature; energy–momentum is conserved; gravity is geometry) become
*identities of the tower*.

This is the bridge from information geometry and simplectic mechanics to
physics: not an analogy table but the statement that \(\Phi\) is the
categorical effective action and that splitting it requires a pre-given
manifold the program declines to assume.

**Note (R′ / §9.7 discharges this section's promissory).** The
informal assertion above that "the relations physics must *posit*
(mass sources curvature; gravity is geometry) become *identities of
the tower*" is discharged in rigorous tiered form by Corollary 9.10
(ledger item R′): the Einstein-like equation \(\delta\mathcal R_{
\text{proj}}\propto T^{(4)}\) is the [C, reduced] sourcing conjecture
in the program's four-pillar accounting of GR, with three pillars
((i) metric = §4 second cumulant; (ii) Bianchi/Filippov = §5 Theorem
5.1; (iv) action-stationarity = this section) inherited [P]. R′
discharges the §3 promissory note; it does not extend the claim
surface.

**Remark 3.2 (External scaffold: the Zhang–Li divergence–symplectic
construction). [P, external precedent]**
Claim 3.1 is the categorical-simplex instance of an established construction in
differential information geometry. For a divergence \(D\) on a manifold \(M\),
Zhang and Li (2013) show the doubled space \(M\times M\) carries a canonical
symplectic form built from the mixed second derivatives of the divergence,
\[
\omega_D \;=\; -\,D_{i,j}\,dx^i\wedge dy^j,
\qquad D_{i,j}=\frac{\partial^2 D}{\partial x^i\,\partial y^j},
\]
that the Fisher–Rao metric is exactly the restriction of the induced metric to
the diagonal \(\Delta_M=\{(x,x)\}\), and that a compatible almost-complex
structure with \(J^2=-I\) exists *iff the divergence is "proper"* (the mixed
partials commute across the variable split, \(D_{i,j}=D_{j,i}\)). For a
\(\Phi\)-divergence the doubled space is Kähler with global potential set by
\(\Phi\). The categorical program is the case where \(D\) is the
Kullback–Leibler divergence of the categorical exponential family and
\(\Phi=\log Z\): the cumulant tower of \(\Phi\) is then exactly this
Zhang–Li potential, the Fisher metric \(g=\nabla^{(2)}\Phi\) is its
diagonal restriction, and the binary symplectic/complex package is the
\(M\times M\) structure, not a primitive. Thus "the four physical objects are
one functional's derivatives" is not only internally derived here but is the
categorical realization of a peer-reviewed divergence-induced symplectic–Kähler
construction. The properness/\(J^2=-I\) condition is invoked again, with teeth,
in Theorems 8.2 and 9.1.

**Remark 3.3 (The "Heisenberg call": why \(\Phi\) is the right object). [P, methodological]**
Claim 3.1 commits the program to an observable-side reading of categorical
mechanics. The log-partition \(\Phi=\log Z\) is a function of the *natural
parameter* \(\theta\) (the dual / source side), and its cumulant tower is a
ladder of observables (centroid, Fisher form, Amari-Chentsov tensor,
higher cumulants), not of states. The state \(p(\theta)\) is recovered only
by the Legendre transform; one cannot recover the cumulant structure from
the state alone without re-introducing \(\Phi\).

This commitment matters because state-side and observable-side analyses
disagree on what classicalization looks like. Dutra, Baldijão & Terra Cunha
(arXiv:2604.24735, Apr 2026) §V close their analysis of Kochen-Specker
classicalization with an explicit "Heisenberg call": the Schrödinger
(state-evolving) picture captures state-dependent contextuality fine, but it
*cannot* see state-independent contextuality classicalization — the latter
requires Heisenberg (observable-evolving) dual-map analysis (their §IV.B,
sequential nesting of \(\mathcal D_p^\dagger\)). The simplicial program's
choice to ground every claim in derivatives of \(\Phi\) rather than in
trajectories of \(p(t)\) is the same methodological choice, made before the
problem is named: cubic mechanics ([17](17.the_cubic_noether_theorem.md),
[24](24.cubic_information_geometric_mechanics_on_the_categorical_simplex.md),
[44](44_quantization_forced_cubic_to_bures_bridge.md)), the Cartan obstruction
([14](14.information_geometric_mechanics_on_the_categorical_simplex.md)), the
conductor blind spot ([34](34_the_conductor_blind_spot.md)), and the closure
certificate (§6 below; [31](31.projection_closure_theory.md)) are all
observable-side statements. The "state-independent" residual that
contextuality literature struggles to classicalize is, in our vocabulary,
the cross-prime carry packet \(M\) (§6.1) — invariant under any
centroid-fixing state-side reduction, removable only by the trajectory-level
(Heisenberg-side) dual-map evolution. DBT26 §IV.B is the explicit
two-qubit instance; §6 below is the general arithmetic statement.

This remark adds no new claim. It records that Claim 3.1's choice of \(\Phi\)
as primitive — rather than the state, the trajectory, or the action — is the
program's standing Heisenberg-picture commitment, and that the
quantum-foundations literature has independently identified this as the
necessary picture for state-independent classicalization analysis.

**Remark 3.4 (T14 promotion: "closure defect" = projected curvature). [P, proved geometric — added v0.2.5; ledger YY].**
The "closure defect" language used throughout §3 (and §5, §6.4, §13) acquires a precise geometric meaning under T14 (Murillo Montero 2026). T14 Theorem 2 (Curvature as Projection-Commutator) proves that for a flat lifted connection $\nabla$ on the apex / ledger algebra and a smooth projection $\Pi_S$ onto a retained packet sector, the curvature of the projected connection $\nabla^{\Pi_S}=\Pi_S\nabla$ is
\[
R^{\Pi_S}(X,Y)=\Pi_S\,d\Pi_S\wedge d\Pi_S\,\Pi_S.
\]
T14 Theorem 3 (Boundary Curvature) then proves that for conductor-packet projections, the *universal support* of the projected curvature defect $\mathcal R_S^{(n)}=(1-\Pi_S-\Pi_1)V_n|_{H_S^{\otimes n}}$ is **exactly the conductor-fusion boundary** $\partial_n S$, sharp. Therefore the §3 "closure defect" of the categorical effective action $\Phi$ is *literally* the projected curvature defect $\mathcal R_S^{(n)}$, and its support is *exactly* the conductor-fusion boundary identified by T11 / T13. **Tier upgrade for §3: [A, structural] → [P, proved geometric].**

T14 Theorem 6 (Equivalence) makes the deeper recognition explicit: in the finite apex setting,
\[
\text{curvature}=\text{projection noncommutation}=\text{boundary leakage}=\text{renormalization source}=\text{Noether defect}=\text{beta source}.
\]
Paper 32's "closure defect / cubic vertex / gravity-adjacent residual / RG-source" cluster is **one finite object** in six languages. See §2.5.6d for the full T14 substrate anchor and §10.6.7n for the explicit channel-level realization at $\mathbb Z/6$.

