## 4. Space and Mass Are the Same Tensor

**Theorem 4.1 (Space = inertia). [P]**
On \(\Delta_R^\circ\) the Fisher metric \(g\) is simultaneously (i) the ruler
that fixes distances and the manifold structure, and (ii) the inertia in the
kinetic core \(\tfrac12 g(v,v)\) of the cubic Lagrangian (Paper 17,
Definition 2.1) and of \(\mathcal E_3\). There is no independent mass primitive
on the simplex; the object multiplying squared velocity is \(g\).

*Proof.* \(g=\nabla^{(2)}\Phi\) is the covariance / second cumulant; it is the
metric by Fisher–Rao and the inertia by inspection of \(\ell_3\) and of the
Paper 28 \(\mathcal E_3 = \tfrac12\sum m_i|v_i|^2 + \tfrac13\sum T h(v\cdot v)\),
where the quadratic core is the Fisher term. No third object is introduced.
\(\blacksquare\)

**Interpretation.** "What do space and mass have in common?" is answered
without metaphor: in a theory with no pre-given space, *space = mass* is the
statement that the metric is the inertia, which is the statement that geometry
is the second cumulant. The identity is invisible in Newtonian setup only
because there spacetime is a container handed in advance and mass is placed
into it.

**Remark 4.2 (External twin: the Koszul–Souriau–Fisher capacity identity).
[P, external precedent]**
Theorem 4.1 is the categorical-simplex instance of an identity already present
in Souriau's Lie-group thermodynamics. There the Hessian of the Massieu
function with respect to the Lie-algebra temperature,
\(I(\beta)=-\partial^2\Phi/\partial\beta^2\), is *simultaneously* the Fisher
information metric and the system's generalized heat capacity; positive
definiteness (the second law / strict convexity of \(\Phi\)) is what makes it a
rigid Riemannian structure. "Metric = inertia = capacity = second cumulant" is
therefore not unique to this program; it is the Koszul–Souriau–Fisher identity
specialized to the categorical exponential family, where \(\beta\leftrightarrow
\theta\) and the Massieu function is \(-\Phi=-\log Z\). This places our cleanest
[P] result on independently established symplectic-thermodynamic footing
without importing the emergent-gravity ontology of §11.1.

