## 6. The Explicit Closure Certificate on \(R=\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\)

This is the technical core. We compute Paper 31's five-slot certificate for the
time-translation projection on the smallest squarefree two-prime row.

### 6.1 The row decomposed

\(R=\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\cong\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}\),
\(Q=6\), \(\dim\Delta_6^\circ=5\). Characters \(\chi_k(x)=e^{2\pi i kx/6}\),
\(k\in\{0,\dots,5\}\). Conductor \(c(k)=6/\gcd(k,6)\):

| \(k\) | \(\gcd(k,6)\) | \(c(k)\) | packet |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 6 | 1 | gauge (killed by \(\sum u_a=0\)) |
| 3 | 3 | 2 | \(A_2\): the \(p{=}2\) axis, 1 real dim |
| 2,4 | 2 | 3 | \(A_3\): the \(p{=}3\) axis, 2 real dims |
| 1,5 | 1 | 6 | \(M\): the squarefree \(2\cdot3\) carry packet, 2 real dims |

So \(H_6=A_2\oplus A_3\oplus M\), dimensions \(1+2+2=5\). \(A_2,A_3\) are the
single-prime axes; \(M\) is the cross/carry packet. \(M\) is exactly the
\(\operatorname{Ext}_{CH}\) support (Paper 13 §11) and the primorial
\(P_2=2\cdot3\) support that Paper 17 §5's Constraint-3 audit stress-tests.

### 6.2 The selection rule, made exact

**Setup and conventions.** Let \(\omega=e^{2\pi i/6}\). The characters of
\(\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\) are \(\chi_k(a)=\omega^{ka}\), \(k,a\in\{0,\dots,5\}\),
and \(e_k\in\mathbb{C}^6\) denotes the vector with components
\((e_k)_a=\omega^{ka}\). Pointwise (Hadamard) product satisfies the grading
\((e_k\odot e_l)_a=\omega^{(k+l)a}=(e_{k+l\bmod 6})_a\). The tangent space
\(H_6=\{u:\sum_a u_a=0\}\) is exactly the zero-mean subspace
\(\chi_0^{\perp}\); hence for every dynamical vector
\(u\in H_6\) (displacement \(h\), velocity \(\dot\eta\)) the zeroth Fourier
component vanishes, \(\widehat u_0=0\). From Paper 13 §7 the Amari–Chentsov
cubic at the centroid \(p_*=(1/6,\dots,1/6)\) is
\(T_{p_*}(u,v,w)=\sum_{a} u_av_aw_a/p_a^2 = 36\sum_{a=0}^{5}u_av_aw_a\).

**Lemma 6.1a (Exact cubic structure constant). [P]**
In the character basis,
\[
T_{klm}\;:=\;T_{p_*}(e_k,e_l,e_m)
\;=\;36\sum_{a=0}^{5}\omega^{(k+l+m)a}
\;=\;216\,\cdot\,\mathbb{1}\!\left[\,k+l+m\equiv 0 \pmod 6\,\right].
\]
*Proof.* The inner geometric sum is \(\sum_{a=0}^{5}\zeta^{a}\) with
\(\zeta=\omega^{k+l+m}\), a sixth root of unity. If \(k+l+m\equiv0\) then
\(\zeta=1\) and the sum is \(6\); otherwise \(\zeta\neq1\),
\(\zeta^{6}=1\), and \(\sum_{a=0}^{5}\zeta^{a}=(\zeta^{6}-1)/(\zeta-1)=0\).
Multiplying by \(36\) gives \(216\) or \(0\). This is precisely the convolution
theorem on \(\widehat{\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}}\): \(T\) is the totally symmetric
structure constant of the group-algebra convolution, nonzero exactly on the
additively closed support. \(\blacksquare\)

\[
\boxed{\;T_{klm}\neq 0 \iff k+l+m\equiv 0 \pmod 6\;}
\]

**Lemma 6.1b (Reality reduction: the real-packet partition is inherited). [P]**
Conjugation \(k\mapsto -k\equiv 6-k\) acts as \(1\leftrightarrow5\),
\(2\leftrightarrow4\), \(3\leftrightarrow3\), \(0\leftrightarrow0\). Each
conductor packet is therefore conjugation-closed:
\(M=\{1,5\}\), \(A_3=\{2,4\}\), \(A_2=\{3\}\) (with \(\chi_3=(-1)^a\) real and
self-conjugate). For real \(u,v,w\in H_6\) the Hermitian symmetry
\(\widehat u_{-k}=\overline{\widehat u_k}\) holds; expanding
\(T(u,v,w)=\sum_{k,l,m}\widehat u_k\widehat v_l\widehat w_m\,T_{klm}\) and using
that \(T_{klm}\) depends only on \(k+l+m\bmod 6\), every real 2-plane spanned by
a conjugate pair stays inside its packet under the contraction. Hence *which
real packets couple* is exactly the complex statement of Lemma 6.1a; the
partition below is basis-independent, not an artifact of the complex
diagonalization. \(\blacksquare\)

**Lemma 6.1c (Off-centroid stability of the support). [P]**
Off the centroid, Paper 13 §7 gives the line-coefficient expansion
\(\bar g^F_{p(t)}(e_\chi,e_\psi)=\sum_{n\ge0}(-t)^n\,\widehat h^{*n}(\psi\chi^{-1})\),
i.e. higher orders enter through convolution powers \(\widehat h^{*n}\).
Convolution on \(\widehat{\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}}\) *adds* frequencies, so it
maps the additively graded algebra into itself and cannot create a triple
violating \(k+l+m\equiv0\). Therefore the **support** of the cubic coupling —
the set of packet triples that can be nonzero — is fixed to all orders in the
centroid expansion by the \(\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\) group grading; only the
*magnitudes* flow off-centroid. The selection rule is a Pontryagin-duality
identity, not a centroid coincidence. (This partially discharges gap G1: the
partition is robust; only coefficient sizes require the §13.1 audit.)
\(\blacksquare\)

**Lemma 6.1 (Packet partition of the cubic interaction — full enumeration).
[P]** Over the dynamical indices \(\{1,2,3,4,5\}\) (the index \(0\) is excluded
by \(\widehat u_0=0\)), the sum \(k+l+m\) ranges in \([3,15]\); the only
multiples of \(6\) attainable are \(6\) and \(12\). The complete list of
closing unordered multisets, with packet signature
(\(M=\{1,5\}\), \(A_3=\{2,4\}\), \(A_2=\{3\}\)) and the symmetric structure
constant from Lemma 6.1a:

| multiset \(\{k,l,m\}\) | sum | \(T_{klm}\) | packet signature | classification |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| \(\{2,2,2\}\) | 6 | 216 | \(A_3,A_3,A_3\) | **closed in \(A_3\)** (pure \(p{=}3\)) |
| \(\{4,4,4\}\) | 12 | 216 | \(A_3,A_3,A_3\) | **closed in \(A_3\)** (conj. of \(\{2,2,2\}\)) |
| \(\{1,1,4\}\) | 6 | 216 | \(M,M,A_3\) | forced through \(M\) |
| \(\{2,5,5\}\) | 12 | 216 | \(A_3,M,M\) | forced through \(M\) (conj. of \(\{1,1,4\}\)) |
| \(\{1,2,3\}\) | 6 | 216 | \(M,A_3,A_2\) | forced through \(M\) (full cross-prime) |
| \(\{3,4,5\}\) | 12 | 216 | \(A_2,A_3,M\) | forced through \(M\) (conj. of \(\{1,2,3\}\)) |

For contrast, the selection rule has bite *inside* a packet too: within
\(A_3=\{2,4\}\) the triples \(\{2,2,4\}\,(8\equiv2)\) and
\(\{2,4,4\}\,(10\equiv4)\) do **not** close, so only the pure \(\{2,2,2\}\)
and \(\{4,4,4\}\) survive even there. Within \(A_2=\{3\}\) the only candidate
is \(\{3,3,3\}\,(9\equiv3\not\equiv0)\); the formal closure \(\{3,3,0\}\)
routes through the gauge mode and is killed by \(\widehat u_0=0\).

*Proof.* Exhaustive: enumerate \(k\le l\le m\) in \(\{1,\dots,5\}\) with
\(k+l+m\in\{6,12\}\). Sum \(6\): \(k=1\Rightarrow(l,m)\in\{(1,4),(2,3)\}\);
\(k=2\Rightarrow(2,2)\); \(k\ge3\) impossible. Sum \(12\):
\(k=2\Rightarrow(5,5)\); \(k=3\Rightarrow(4,5)\); \(k=4\Rightarrow(4,4)\);
\(k\in\{1,5\}\) impossible by range. This yields exactly the six rows; packet
signatures read off the membership map; values from Lemma 6.1a. \(\blacksquare\)

**Corollary 6.1d (Three exact consequences). [P]**
From the enumeration, with no remaining freedom:

1. **\(A_3\) self-closure is exactly two triples:** \(\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}\).
   The \(p{=}3\) axis carries a closed, nonempty cubic self-interaction.
2. **\(A_2\) is cubically isolated.** No closing triple has all legs in
   \(A_2\cup\{0\}\): \(\{3,3,3\}\not\equiv0\) and \(\{3,3,0\}\) is gauge-killed.
   Moreover, *every* closing triple containing the index \(3\) — namely
   \(\{1,2,3\}\) and \(\{3,4,5\}\) — necessarily carries an \(M\)-leg
   (\(1\) or \(5\)). The \(p{=}2\) axis cannot participate in any cubic
   coupling except through \(M\). (Cross-prime forcing, exactly: \(3{+}2\equiv
   -1\Rightarrow\) third leg \(\equiv1\in M\); \(3{+}4\equiv1\Rightarrow\)
   third leg \(\equiv5\in M\).)
3. **\(\Pi_\tau\) survival set is exactly \(\{\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}\}\).** A term
   survives \(\Pi_\tau\) (which annihilates all \(M\)-components) iff no leg lies
   in \(M\); of the six rows only the two pure-\(A_3\) rows qualify. Every other
   closing triple has an \(M\)-leg, hence the discarded part
   \(T_{\mathrm{cross}}\) vanishes whenever the \(M\)-components vanish — i.e.
   the residual is *supported on \(M\)* (conductor 6), not merely
   correlated with it. \(\blacksquare\)

Thus the cubic interaction splits exactly and provably as
\[
\underbrace{\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}}_{\text{closed in }A_3,\ \Pi_\tau\text{-surviving}}
\;\oplus\;
\underbrace{\varnothing}_{A_2\text{ cubically isolated}}
\;\oplus\;
\underbrace{\{1,1,4\},\{2,5,5\},\{1,2,3\},\{3,4,5\}}_{\text{all carry an }M\text{-leg};\ \Pi_\tau\text{-annihilated; residual on conductor 6}}.
\]

### 6.2A The four-thread arithmetic root

The exhaustive enumeration above has a single congruence as its load-bearing input. Calling it out as a lemma makes the dependency graph of four otherwise-independent corpus results visible at once.

**Lemma 6.2 (The four-thread arithmetic root). [P]**
The congruence
\[
3 + 3 + 3 \;=\; 9 \;\equiv\; 3 \;\not\equiv\; 0 \pmod 6,
\]
combined with the selection rule \(T_{k\ell m}\neq 0 \iff k+\ell+m \equiv 0 \pmod 6\) (Lemma 6.1a), yields two structural facts already used implicitly in Lemma 6.1 / Corollary 6.1d:

**(L1)** *Pure-\(A_2\) cubic vacuity.* Since \(A_2=\{3\}\), the only candidate pure-\(A_2\) closing multiset is \(\{3,3,3\}\). By the congruence it fails to close, so \(T_{p_*}(u,v,w)=0\) for every \(u,v,w\in A_2\).

**(L2)** *Forced \(M\)-mediation of every \(A_2\)-leg coupling.* A closing triple containing the leg \(k=3\) requires \(\ell+m\equiv 3\pmod 6\) with \(\ell,m\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}\). The exhaustive solutions are \(\{3,1,2\}\) and \(\{3,4,5\}\) (the case \(\{3,3,0\}\) is gauge-killed by \(\widehat u_0=0\)). Each carries an \(M\)-leg (\(1\) or \(5\)).

*Proof.* (L1) is the congruence. (L2) is the exhaustive enumeration on \(\{1,\dots,5\}^2\) already tabulated in Lemma 6.1. \(\square\)

**Corollary 6.3 (Four threads, one root). [P]**
Each of the following four corpus results — drawn from independent reaches of the program — is a direct consequence of (L1) and (L2):

**(T1) The simplex-mass-ratio audit closes negative on \(\mathbb{Z}/6\) (Paper 20 OQ1).** By Paper 20 Definition 2.1, \(m^2_{\text{simplex}}(\mathbf C)\) is the leading \(T^{(4)}\) coefficient in the conductor-total sector \(\mathbf C\). By (L1) the cubic self-energy of \(A_2\) vanishes identically, so \(m^2_{\text{simplex}}(A_2)=0\) exactly. Every ordered ratio \(\mathcal Y(A_2,\mathbf C')\) is \(0\); every \(\mathcal Y(\mathbf C',A_2)\) is \(\infty\) — the *degenerate stated branch*, not a non-trivial conductor-fixed pure number. The packet \(M=\{1,5\}\) is similarly cubically inert in pure-\(M\) self-coupling (\(\{1,1,1\}, \{5,5,5\}\) both have sum \(\equiv 3\not\equiv 0\)). The \(\mathbb{Z}/6\) row furnishes no non-trivial simplex-mass ratio.

**(T2) The single external chirality bit is color-protected (Paper 16 §3; Paper 33 §3).** A direct \(A_2\!\cdot\!A_3\!\cdot\!A_3\) or \(A_2\!\cdot\!A_2\!\cdot\!A_3\) trilinear vertex would supply an internal cross-prime channel between the parity-character sector and the cube-root sector. By (L2) every \(A_2\)-leg closing triple is \(\{3,1,2\}\) or \(\{3,4,5\}\) and carries an \(M\)-leg; no direct \(A_2\)–\(A_3\) vertex exists. The cross-prime channel is forced through \(M\), the same conductor-6 packet supporting the residual / jerk. The KO-real-structure \(\mathbb{Z}_2\) of finite-Standard-Model spectral triples therefore cannot be produced internally by any real-cone-preserving Bures lift; it remains the single external bit of the Paper 33 Chirality Boundary, with count \(2^{c-1}=1\) at \(c=2\) realized as the count of \(M\)-mediated cross-prime channels modulo packet conjugation.

**(T3) Friston's gradient-vs-least-action fork resolves sector-wise on the categorical-belief simplex \(\mathbb{Z}/6\). [P] structural (audit FEP-3, 2026-05-20).** Friston's monograph poses, as an open fork of deep active inference, whether the belief flow is a gradient descent on free energy or a least-action / Lagrangian process. On the canonical discrete-state instance the four conditions trace immediately: (a) Corollary 6.1d.1 (\(A_3\) self-closes on \(\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}\) — the conserved sector is non-trivially closed); (b) (L1) (no pure-\(A_2\) classical conserved cubic charge); (c) (L2) (the 12 ordered \((A_2,A_3,M)\) cross-couplings, zero exceptions); (d) the resulting \(2+18=20\) partition. The least-action description survives on the Cartan/single-prime sector; the complement is forced to be the gradient-like leakage channel; the boundary is the positive-cone-preservation condition of Paper 14 Corollary 2.4. **Audit FEP-3** ([`empirical/reports/audit_fep3_settlement.md`](empirical/reports/audit_fep3_settlement.md), 2026-05-20) verified the metriplectic decomposition at machine precision on \(D(\mathbb{C}^6)\): variational free-energy decomposes additively as \(F = F_V + F_M\) with \(F_V = I_{\text{real}}\) Hamiltonian (V-supported, reversible, conserved by V-flow) and \(F_M = \alpha^2 \cdot (m^2_{\text{simplex}})^2\) gradient-of-entropy (M-supported, monotone-increasing under M-component flow, irreversible). Four predicates confirmed: V-rays Hamiltonian; M-rays gradient-of-entropy; V ⊥ M cross-coupling zero at every order (audit \(\delta\) specialized); \(F = F_V + F_M\) additive with no interaction term. The arrow of time on the categorical simplex's conductor lattice is the direction of \(\nabla F_M\). The contribution to the FEP stack is a *finite, exact, reproducible resolution of an open fork on the canonical instance*; structural [P] established on \(\mathbb{Z}/6\). Empirical biological-system applicability remains [C, reduced] (deferred for ground-truth active-inference data).

**(T4) The conductor-misspecification certificate for second-order preconditioning on ring-structured categorical heads (Paper 12; cf. beyond_smooth §1).** Of the 20 closing triples on \(\{1,\dots,5\}\), exactly 2 are pure-packet (\(\{2,2,2\},\{4,4,4\}\)) and 18 are cross-packet, of which 12 are \((A_2,A_3,M)\) by (L2) and 6 are \((A_3,M,M)\) by Lemma 6.1. The quadratic Fisher form \(g_{p_*}\) is packet-block-diagonal by character orthogonality and carries \(0\) of the 18 cross-packet entries; the Amari–Chentsov cubic \(T_{p_*}\) carries all 18. Therefore any preconditioner built from \(g\) alone — natural gradient, K-FAC, Gauss–Newton, Adam's diagonal empirical Fisher, the NTK Gram — is provably blind to a coupling the next cumulant floor demonstrably carries. The structural ratio "cross-packet content missed by any second-order preconditioner on a ring-structured categorical head" is \(18/20=9/10\) on this row, with the \(A_2\)-involving moiety (\(12/18=2/3\)) forced by (L2). The certificate is misspecification-only; it makes no claim about any trained model's trajectory. \(\square\)

**Remark 6.4 (Scope and the primorial sequel).** The four threads come from independent reaches of the corpus — a constants audit (Paper 20), a spectral-triple chirality result (Paper 16), an outbound contribution to active inference (Friston), and a deep-learning misspecification statement (Paper 12 + beyond_smooth). Corollary 6.3 reduces them to a single congruence on \(\mathbb{Z}/6\), raising the standing of the coincidence from "four parallel results" to *jointly* arithmetic: one finite fact, four consequences. On the next squarefree primorial \(\mathbb{Z}/30=2\cdot3\cdot5\) the analogous \(p=2\) self-vacuity is \(15+15+15=45\equiv 15\not\equiv 0\pmod{30}\), again non-closing; whether the four threads continue to co-decide at quartic order is the open primorial-ladder question, recorded here as the natural sequel and as the falsifier of generalization, not as a result.

### 6.3 The projection and the residual

Define the time-translation projection onto the closure-valid macrovariable
subalgebra (the within-single-prime-packet phase actions, i.e. the Paper 17
Cartan directions):
\[
\Pi_\tau:\ H_6\longrightarrow A_2\oplus A_3,\qquad \Pi_\tau\big|_M=0.
\]
\(\Pi_\tau\) retains the two abelian \(\mathfrak u(1)\) generators (phase on
\(A_2\), phase on \(A_3\)) — the exactly two cubic charges Paper 17 says
exhaust the classical dictionary — and discards \(M\). The whole \(W\) is the
e-geodesic / unitary orbit upstairs; \(\Pi_\tau\) is its diagonal classical
shadow in Paper 20's sense.

Write \(\eta=\eta_A+\eta_M\). At the centroid \(g_{p_*}=Q\,I_H\), packets
orthogonal, so the kinetic term splits and survives \(\Pi_\tau\) as
\(\tfrac12 g(\dot\eta_A,\dot\eta_A)\) with no defect. By Lemma 6.1 the cubic
term decomposes as
\[
T(h,\dot\eta,\dot\eta)=
\underbrace{T_{A_3}}_{\text{within }A_3,\ \text{closes}}
+\underbrace{0}_{A_2\text{ inert}}
+\underbrace{T_{\mathrm{cross}}}_{\text{every term has an }M\text{-leg}}.
\]
\(\Pi_\tau\) annihilates \(T_{\mathrm{cross}}\). Therefore

**Theorem 6.2 (Localized time-translation defect). [P]**
\[
\frac{d}{dt}\,\mathcal E_3\Big|_{\Pi_\tau}
= 0 \;-\; \frac{d}{dt}\Big[\tfrac13 T_{\mathrm{cross}}(h,\dot\eta,\dot\eta)\Big]
= -\tfrac13\,\nabla^{(e)}T\big|_M
\;\equiv\; -\tfrac13\,T^{(4)}\big|_M .
\]
The non-conservation of the projected Noether energy is exactly the jerk,
supported entirely on the conductor-6 packet \(M\). It is Paper 30's
\(R^{(4)}\), Paper 20's simplex-mass term, and \(\operatorname{Ext}_{CH}\), and
Lemma 6.1 names the exact Fourier modes it occupies. \(\blacksquare\)

### 6.4 The certificate tuple \(\mathcal C_W(\Pi_\tau;T_\tau,H,K{=}3)\)

Per Paper 31 Definition 5.1:

- **\(\mathsf P^{\Pi_\tau}_W\) (retained participation). [P]** The single-prime
  packets \(A_2,A_3\) and their phase actions; equivalently the abelian Cartan
  \(\mathfrak u(1)^2\) of Paper 17 Corollary 2.4. Kinetic core and within-\(A_3\)
  cubic charge survive intact. This is the "two-body weak-field-like" sector:
  single-prime, self-closing, no carry.
- **\(\mathsf R^{\Pi_\tau}_W\) (typed residual). [P]** Exactly
  \(\tfrac13 T_{\mathrm{cross}}\) and its derivative \(-\tfrac13 T^{(4)}|_M\),
  supported on \(M=\{\chi_1,\chi_5\}\), conductor 6. Typed: *cross-prime cubic
  carry / first overflow pointer \(R^{(4)}\)*. \(= \operatorname{Ext}_{CH}\).
- **\(\mathsf B^{\Pi_\tau}_W\) (bound). [P/A]**
  \(\|R^{\Pi_\tau}\|\le \tfrac13\|T|_M\|\,\|h_M\|\,\|\dot\eta\|^2
  \le C_6\|h\|\,\|\dot\eta\|^2\), \(C_6\) the operator norm of the convolution
  restricted to \(M\) (finite; \(\dim M=2\)). Finiteness is the Marcinkiewicz
  bounded-support fact (Papers 21, 22): the cumulant tower on a finite
  squarefree row has bounded support, so the carry cannot run away. Persistence
  of the bound under prime-power depth is **[A]** (§13.1).
- **\(\mathsf S^{\Pi_\tau}_W\) (stability). [P/A]** Recurrent, non-chaotic,
  Fisher-volume-preserving; \(M\) is a fixed 2-dimensional packet the flow
  cannot pump unboundedly (Paper 19). **Non-tachyonic**: the residual energy is
  \(\ge0\), and this is *the same positivity* as Paper 22's Weil/Noether form
  on the \(P_2=2\cdot3\) row at cubic order (Theorem 7.1). Sign-definiteness
  under depth is **[A]** (§13.1).
- **\(\mathsf F^{\Pi_\tau}_W\) (falsifiers). [P]** (i) *Localization*: any
  measured time-translation residual in \(A_2\) or \(A_3\) refutes the
  derivation — forbidden by Lemma 6.1. (ii) *Depth*: under
  \(\mathbb{Z}/6\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}/12,\mathbb{Z}/18\) the residual must
  stay on the squarefree conductor-6 mode; a new depth-induced carry axis
  refutes time-translation closure on the row. (iii) *Boundedness/stability*:
  unbounded \(\|R^{\Pi_\tau}\|\), a negative conductor-6 Noether form, or
  chaotic long-time behavior on \(M\) refutes the certificate and
  simultaneously breaks Paper 22's positivity route.

**Verdict.**
\[
\Pi_\tau\in\operatorname{ClValid}(W,T_\tau,H,K{=}3)
\iff \text{dynamics confined to }A_2\oplus A_3.
\]
The closure-valid locus is *exactly* the abelian Cartan \(\mathfrak u(1)^2\) —
re-deriving Paper 17 from projection closure. The typed bounded residual is
*exactly* the conductor-6 carry: the gravity/dark-matter-adjacent term, now
arithmetically localized and computable. Crucially the closure-failure locus
does **not** coincide with the integrability locus \(\{\nabla^{(e)}T=0\}\)
(Paper 17 Theorem 3.1) — it coincides with the conductor-6 *packet*. The
gravity-adjacent term is selected by *arithmetic* (which primes mix), not by
*dynamics* (where the orbit sits). That is the new statement.

