## 8. Dimensional Selection: Why an Inertial Projector Closes in 3

**Definition 8.1 (Inertial projector).**
An inertial projector is a projection \(\Pi\) whose closure-valid self-model
has: a single conserved energy from a single Hamiltonian; a single rotation
axis (angular momentum representable as a vector); and a complex structure
\(J^2=-I\) organizing transverse rotation. This is precisely the binary
symplectic package. A *mass-structured being* is, by Theorem 4.1, an inertial
projector: having a nondegenerate second cumulant is having inertia.

**Theorem 8.2 (Dimensional selection, route 1: Hodge/\(\mathfrak{so}\)). [P]**
The binary symplectic package is native to exactly one row of the Fisher-volume
hierarchy. The three structural facts: \(\dim\mathfrak{so}(n)=n(n-1)/2=n\)
only at \(n=3\); \(\Lambda^2(\mathbb R^n)\cong\mathbb R^n\) only at \(n=3\);
\(K(u)\) restricts to \(J^2=-I\) on \(u^\perp\) only in the dim-3 row (Paper 13
§2). Paper 13's worked rows give the cost elsewhere: \(Q=3\) (dim 2) is
degenerate area mechanics; \(Q\ge5\) (dim \(\ge4\)) has native momentum form of
degree \(Q-2\ge3\), no single 2-form, \(Q-2\ge3\) simultaneous independent
macro-Hamiltonians, no single rotation axis. An inertial projector — one
energy, one Hamiltonian, one spin axis — can therefore have a closure-valid
self-model only at \(Q=4\), spatial dimension 3. \(\blacksquare\)

**Remark 8.2a (External anchor for the \(J^2=-I\) singularity).
[P, external precedent]**
The \(J^2=-I\) clause of Theorem 8.2 is not only the Paper 13 §2 cross-product
observation; it coincides with the Zhang–Li compatibility condition of
Remark 3.2: a divergence-induced almost-complex structure with \(J^2=-I\)
exists *only* when the divergence is proper, and on the categorical doubled
space the row at which the diagonal restriction carries a single such \(J\)
acting as a genuine quarter-turn is exactly the dim-3 row. Dimensional
selection is therefore the categorical instance of an established
divergence-geometry compatibility constraint, not a coincidence of small-\(n\)
linear algebra alone. This is the second of the two places Remark 3.2 said the
properness condition would return with teeth.

**Theorem 8.3 (Dimensional selection, route 2: conductor count). [P]**
On the cubic two-prime row (§6), the closure-valid block is
\(A_2\oplus A_3\), real dimension \(1+2=3\); the complementary carry packet
\(M\) has real dimension \(2\) and is *not* perceived as space — it is the
gravity-adjacent residual of Theorem 6.2. An independent route therefore yields
the same closure-valid spatial dimension, 3, and identifies the non-spatial
remainder as the gravity term. \(\blacksquare\)

**Corollary 8.4 (Why 3D, answered). [P]**
Experiencing space as three-dimensional is the closure certificate of being an
inertial projector — equivalently of having mass. The two questions of this
session ("why 3D?" and "what is the gravity residual?") have one answer: the
\(3+2\) split of the conductor block is simultaneously the spatial/residual
split.

**Scope.** This is a closure-selection statement: it explains why an inertial
projector closes in dimension 3 and nowhere else in the hierarchy. It does not
derive inertia from nothing (Theorem 4.1 makes "why inertia" near-tautological:
any nondegenerate Fisher metric *is* inertia) and it does not by itself fix the
Lorentzian signature — that is §9.

