## 9. Signature Is a Lift Phenomenon

### 9.1 The classical signature is \((3,1)\) but Galilean

There is no fourth velocity component: \(v=v_{A_2}\oplus v_{A_3}\), three
dimensions; time is the parameter the flow is written in, not a coordinate. So
\(\mathcal E_3\) is a positive-definite form in three spatial velocities (Fisher
positivity); the real simplex face-value gives Euclidean-3 plus an external
clock, matching Paper 20 §4's disclaimer. The signature lives not in
\(\mathcal E_3\) but in the **Poincaré–Cartan one-form** the Noether
construction itself produces:
\[
\Theta=\frac{\partial\ell_3}{\partial v}\cdot d\eta-\mathcal E_3\,dt
\;=\;\underbrace{g(v,\cdot)\,d\eta}_{(+,+,+)\ \text{Fisher on }A_2\oplus A_3}
\;\underbrace{-\;\mathcal E_3\,dt}_{(-)\ \text{the }-\ell_3\text{ Legendre sign}}.
\]

**Theorem 9.1 (Forced classical \((3,1)\)). [P]**
\(d\Theta\) induces, on the extended tangent space (three Fisher spatial
directions ⊕ the flow–time direction), the signature
\[
\boxed{(\,+,\,+,\,+\,;\,-\,)}.
\]
The three plus signs are Fisher positivity on the closure-valid block
(Theorem 8.3); the minus is the literal \(-\ell_3\) / \(-\mathcal E\,dt\) in the
*definition* of the Noether energy (Paper 17 Definition 2.1). The temporal
minus is not a negative metric direction; it is the contact/Legendre sign of
energy being conjugate to the flow parameter. Space's "3" and time's "1" do not
share a metric block — the 3 is Fisher-definite, the 1 is Legendre-dual; the
relative sign between them is contact-geometric, not Riemannian. \(\blacksquare\)

**Remark 9.1a (The \(3{+}1\) dimension is inherited from the \(\mathbb H\)
terminator).** The spatial dimension \(3\) and the time dimension \(1\) are not
two independent free choices. The space of \(2\times 2\) Hermitian matrices on
the \(\mathbb C^2\) leg of the CRT factorization (Paper 18 §5) has *real*
dimension \(4=\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathbb H\) by the Hurwitz / Cayley–Dickson
terminator (Paper 18 Theorem 6.2: the norm-closed real ladder
\(\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H,\mathbb O\) stops, no fifth rung). The
canonical \(1\,(\text{trace})+3\,(\text{traceless})\) decomposition of a
\(2\times 2\) Hermitian matrix is the \(1\) real \(+\,3\) imaginary axes of
the quaternion. The \(3{+}1\) split of \(\Theta\) is therefore the same
\(3{+}1\) the program already owns at [Certain] tier — inherited from a
finished theorem, not newly assumed; cf. ledger item S′ on the broader
ℂ²-leg face collapse.

**Remark 9.1b (The Riemannian pre-lift geometry is constant-curvature, not
Einsteinian).** The Fisher metric on \(\Delta_R^\circ\), pulled back through
the Hellinger embedding (Paper 13 Definition 14.6A), is a sector of a round
sphere of constant positive curvature in Riemannian signature; it is a *fixed*
geometry with no back-reaction of the matter content on \(g\). What Theorem 9.1
gives is therefore the *signature* of \(d\Theta\) on this fixed background,
not a dynamical metric. The promotion to a *sourced* curvature is opened as
the Einstein-like equation R′ (§5.A, §12), strictly separated from the
signature statement here. Lemma 9.2's "Galilean, not Lorentzian" tier
operates on the same fixed background; the two scope statements reinforce
rather than duplicate.

**Lemma 9.2 (But Galilean, not Lorentzian). [P]**
With \(\pi=\partial_v\ell_3=g(v)+T(h,v)\), to leading order
\(\mathcal E_3=\tfrac12\langle\pi,g^{-1}\pi\rangle+\tfrac13
T(h,g^{-1}\pi,g^{-1}\pi)\): energy linear, momentum quadratic. That is a
paraboloid — the Galilean/Newtonian dispersion (absolute time). No classical
Lorentz structure, fully consistent with the series. \(\blacksquare\)

### 9.2 The Lorentzian sharpening

**Theorem 9.3 (Hyperboloid requires quadratic energy). [P]**
A genuine two-sheeted hyperboloid \(\mathcal E^2-|\pi|^2=m^2\), boost
invariance, and a light cone require \(\mathcal E\) to enter the invariant
*quadratically*, not linearly. \(\blacksquare\)

**Conjecture 9.4 (Signature is the Bures lift). [C]**
The unique place in the program where the energy invariant becomes quadratic is
the Bures density-matrix lift (Paper 18), where energy is \(\operatorname{tr}
(H\rho)\) and the second-order structure is the Bures metric. We conjecture that
on the lifted conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3 block the level set
\(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)=E\) is a genuine two-sheeted hyperboloid invariant
under a 3-parameter group completing the Cartan, so that Lorentzian \(3+1\),
the nonabelian gauge restoration of Paper 18, and the imaginary leakage energy
of Paper 20 are *one and the same lift*. Reduced to the single verification of
§13.2. Falsifier: if the lifted level set is an ellipsoid (stays Euclidean) or
the invariance group is wrong-dimensional, the framework is intrinsically
Euclidean-4 — itself a stated, major structural result (§12, item E).

**Remark 9.5 (One lift, three faces).** Conjecture 9.4 asserts that gauge
restoration, the imaginary \(i\) (\(=K(u)^2{=}{-}I\), itself dim-3-row-only,
Paper 13 §2), and Lorentz signature are not three lift consequences but three
faces of one lift event. Stated as [C], reduced, and on the table so it cannot
be claimed independently elsewhere.

**Remark 9.6 (External precedent for the lift, not a proof). [C-support]**
That a *bona fide* time generator should appear only on a non-commuting lifted
algebra, not classically, is a recognized structure: Tomita–Takesaki modular
theory and the Connes–Rovelli thermal-time hypothesis give, for a faithful
normal state on a von Neumann algebra, a canonical one-parameter modular flow
generated by \(-\log\rho\) — present precisely because the algebra is
non-commuting and absent on a commuting (classical) restriction. This is *not*
invoked as evidence that Conjecture 9.4 is true; it is invoked to show that the
move "the genuine temporal/Lorentzian structure lives at the Bures
non-commuting lift, while the classical real-simplex level is Galilean" is a
respected structural pattern rather than an ad hoc rescue. Tag: [C-support],
precedent only; the verdict remains the §13.2 audit.

### 9.3 The invariant speed as the fourth face of the lift

**Corollary 9.7 (The invariant speed is a lift phenomenon; \(c\equiv1\) is
structural, not a unit choice).**

*Part 1 — \(c\equiv1\) is forced, [P-adjacent given §4, §5, §9.1].* The spatial
block of \(\Theta\) is the Fisher metric (the second cumulant; space = inertia,
Theorem 4.1) and the temporal block is the Legendre \(-\mathcal E_3\,dt\) of the
*same* potential's time-translation sector (Theorem 5.1, §9.1). Two covariant
derivatives of one functional \(\Phi\) admit no independent relative
normalization: there is no separate ruler and no separate clock between which a
conversion factor could be free. Hence the spatial–temporal conversion constant
is structurally unity. In this framework \(c=1\) is therefore not a convention
but a consequence of the one-functional structure — the statement that the two
faces of \(\Phi\) share a normalization. Equivalently: "the speed of causality"
is the rate of the time-translation sector relative to the spatial sector, and
it is fixed to 1 because both sectors are the same \(\Phi\).

*Part 2 — a finite invariant speed exists only at the lift, [C], reduced to
§13.2.* By Lemma 9.2 the classical real-simplex dispersion is parabolic
(\(\mathcal E_3\) linear, momentum quadratic): a parabola has no asymptote, so
the classical (Galilean) regime has *no* finite causal speed limit at all —
Newtonian mass admits no \(c\). By Theorem 9.3 a hyperbolic mass-shell
\(\mathcal E^2-|\pi|^2=m^2\) requires energy to enter quadratically, which
occurs only at the Bures lift (Conjecture 9.4). A hyperboloid *has* asymptotes;
its asymptotic slope *is* the invariant speed. Therefore the finite invariant
speed is created — not merely revealed — by the lift, as the asymptote of the
energy–momentum hyperboloid, and its internal value is 1 because the lift
introduces no new scale: it only quadratizes the pre-existing \(\mathcal E_3\).
"\(c\) is the time-translation limit of mass" is then literally exact: it is the
asymptote of the mass-shell, and the mass-shell acquires an asymptote precisely
at the completion that delivers the light cone, gauge restoration, and the
imaginary energy.

*Part 3 — the \(i\)-face is the sibling of the signature face; both invariants
are derived, not postulated, [A] reduced (§9.4).* The same one-functional logic
that fixes \(c\) on the signature face has a precise counterpart on the
imaginary-\(i\) face. The SLD/Bures metric of Paper 18 §4 is the unique
monotone metric whose conservative Nambu–Bures flow is unitary von Neumann
evolution \(\dot\rho=-i[H,\rho]\); the Mandelstam–Tamm/Bures quantum speed
limit specializes to that flow as *Bures distinguishability rate*
\(=\) *energy spread* \(/\,\hbar\). The constant \(\hbar\) is therefore the
conversion between the program's own statistical-distinguishability clock and
the unitary phase clock — the \(i\)-face's slope exactly as \(c\) is the
signature face's asymptote slope. Faces (3) and (4) of the lift therefore
carry, respectively, ℏ and \(c\), each derived to exist, derived to be
invariant, and structurally pinned — while neither's dimensionful value is
predicted (§11.2 firewall). The single lift carries **two paired conversion
invariants, one per face**: \(c\) (space ↔ time) on the signature face, ℏ
(Fisher clock ↔ phase clock) on the \(i\)-face. The detailed structure is in
§9.4.

*Constants cross-reference (firewall-binding).* Having named both paired
invariants, the *only* firewall-admissible constants question the pair
opens is which scale-free conductor/cumulant combinations are structurally
fixed — three candidate targets pre-enumerated in §11.2.1 (ledger item
U′); the Compton wavelength \(\bar\lambda_C=\hbar/(mc)\) is *not* one of
them and is named there as the firewall trap of the pair, exactly as the
metre/second value of \(c\) is excluded here. No magnitude of \(c\), of
\(\hbar\), or of any length or mass derived from them is ever a deliverable
of this program.

*Causal cross-reference (§9.6 / item W′).* The §13.2A local-Casimir
asymptote locus where \(c\) is read **is the null cone of Corollary
9.9** — the exactly-vanishing projection-closure residual at the
boundary of Face A's local-tangent hyperboloid, where the leakage
balance is realized. Reading the same locus from the speed-limit side
gives \(c\); reading it from the causal-trichotomy side gives the null
class. One locus, two readings.

*Consequence.* Conjecture 9.4's "one lift, three faces" (Remark 9.5) is
upgraded to **one lift, four faces**: Lorentzian signature, nonabelian gauge
restoration, the imaginary \(i=K(u)^2{=}{-}I\), and *the existence and
unit-value of a finite invariant speed*. Crucially this adds **no new audit**:
the asymptotic slope of the level set \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)=E\) is a free
read-off from the same two-sheeted-hyperboloid computation already specified in
§13.2 / Appendix F. The deliverable is explicitly *structural, not numerical*:
the framework derives that an invariant speed exists, is frame-independent, is
the hyperboloid asymptote, and equals 1 in internal units — it does **not**
predict the value of \(c\) in any physical unit, which remains outside the
no-scale firewall by construction (cf. §11, the dimensional firewall). What
Special Relativity *postulates* (an invariant speed; its constancy), this
program would *derive*; what no program can claim from a scale-free substrate
(its metric value) is permanently out of scope. \(\square\)

**Closing note (§13.2 + §13.2A audit SETTLEMENT — two-level outcome).**
Part 2 of this corollary was the [C, reduced] arm of items K / J′ of the
§12 ledger, conditional on the §13.2 Bures-dispersion audit. The audit has
been run in two stages:

*Stage 1 — Original §13.2 (level set of \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\)).* The
literal reading returned the Euclidean-4 branch: the level set is convex,
compact, and connected (not a two-sheeted hyperboloid); the Bures metric is
positive-definite Riemannian; the manifold-level symmetry group \(U(3)\) is
compact, so no non-compact Lorentzian boost group acts on \(D(V)\). At the
manifold/global level, the framework is Euclidean-4 (item L confirmed).

*Stage 2 — Reformulated §13.2A (the "missing energy term").* The
original specification was structurally inadequate because
\(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\) is *linear* in \(\rho\) — linear functions on
convex compact domains have affine slices as level sets, never quadric
surfaces. The right object is the *quadratic* Casimir
\(I(\rho)=\langle H\rangle^2-\sum_a\langle P_a\rangle^2\) (the "missing
energy term" being the joint energy–momentum quadratic structure, not a
single linear-in-\(\rho\) energy). The reformulated audit checks the
Hessian of \(I\) at \(\rho_*\) and finds, for every HS-orthogonal
\((H,P_1,P_2,P_3)\) drawn from the natural \(\mathfrak{su}(3)\) generators
on \(V\), Hessian signature \((1,3,4)\) on the 8-dim tangent space — a
genuine Lorentzian \((1,3)\) sub-bundle on \(\operatorname{span}\{H,P_a\}\),
with the leading-order level set a two-sheeted hyperboloid at \(\rho_*\).
Item K is partially resurrected at the *local tangent level*.

*Consequence — the two-level picture.* The Bures lift on the conductor-2 ⊕
conductor-3 block carries two coexisting geometric structures: (i) the
*global* Riemannian Bures metric (positive-definite, manifold-level); and
(ii) a *local* Lorentzian \((1,3)\) Casimir-Hessian quadratic form on a
4-dim tangent sub-bundle at \(\rho_*\). Both are honest features of the
same lift; the original matched-pair K/L framing was binary and too coarse
to capture this. Part 1 of the corollary (\(c\equiv1\) as structural unity)
stands unconditionally. Face (2) (gauge) and face (3) (the \(i\)-face with
invariant \(\hbar\), item T′) stand unconditionally. Faces (1) (Lorentzian
signature) and (4) (invariant speed \(c\)) are refuted at the manifold
level but confirmed at the local tangent level — they live as Casimir-
Hessian Lorentzian sub-bundle structures, not as global Lie-group actions.
See §13.2 and §13.2A for the two audit settlements and §12 items K, L, J′,
S′ for the two-level tier transitions. The Galilean / fixed-Riemannian
background of Lemma 9.2 and §9.1 holds at the manifold level; the
infinitesimal Lorentzian structure of §13.2A sits on top of it.

**Remark 9.8 (The ℂ²-leg real-form duality: algebraic preserved, geometric
realization refuted by §13.2). [SETTLED — partial, ledger item S′.]**
A two-layer statement, decided differently by the §13.2 audit.

*Algebraic layer (preserved unconditionally).* On the \(\mathbb Z/2\)
(i.e. \(\mathbb C^2\)) CRT leg of the
\(\mathbb C[\mathbb Z/6]\cong\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3\) decomposition
(Paper 18 §5), the compact real form
\(\mathfrak{su}(2)\cong\mathrm{Spin}(3)\) (weak isospin) and the non-compact
real form \(\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\cong\mathrm{Spin}(3,1)\) (Lorentz
double cover, acting on \(2\times 2\) Hermitians with
\(\det=(x^0)^2-|\mathbf x|^2\)) are *two real forms of the single
complexification* \(\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb C)_{\mathbb C}\); the canonical
\(1\,(\text{trace})+3\,(\text{traceless})\) decomposition of a \(2\times 2\)
Hermitian matches the \(1\) real \(+\,3\) imaginary axes of the quaternion
(Paper 18 Theorem 6.2, the \(\mathbb H\) terminator). These are
Lie-algebra / division-algebra facts and stand unconditionally. The
sharpening of Paper 33 Q4 — that the chirality \(\mathbb Z_2\) is the
*single sign* the real-form choice does not fix — inherits this layer and
stands.

*Geometric realization (two-level: globally refuted by §13.2, locally
confirmed by §13.2A).* The further conjecture that the Bures lift IS the
compact→non-compact passage on the conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3 block of
\(D(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3)\) splits at two levels:

*Manifold level (globally refuted).* The §13.2 audit's diagnostic tests
(B), (C), (D) show that the lift produces compact and Riemannian structure
(compact level set, positive-definite Bures metric \(3\cdot I_8\) at
\(\rho_*\), compact toric stabilizer \(U(1)^3\)); the manifold-level
symmetry group is \(U(3)\), compact, with no non-compact Lorentzian
subgroup. No \(\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\) Lie-group action exists on
\(D(V)\) globally. The pre-claimed falsifier of the
"compact \(\mathfrak{su}(2)\) without
\(\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\) globally" form fires.

*Local tangent level (confirmed by §13.2A).* The Hessian of the Casimir
\(\langle H\rangle^2-\sum_a\langle P_a\rangle^2\) at \(\rho_*\) has
signature \((1,3,4)\) on the 8-dim tangent space, giving a Lorentzian
\((1,3)\) quadratic form on the 4-dim sub-bundle
\(\operatorname{span}\{H,P_a\}\). The 4-dim Lorentz-signature sub-bundle
matches the \(1+3\) decomposition of \(2\times 2\) Hermitians of the
\(\mathbb H\) terminator; the \(\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\) action
\(M\mapsto AMA^\dagger\) preserves the Minkowski norm
\(\det M=(x^0)^2-|\mathbf x|^2\) — precisely the quadratic form of the
local sub-bundle. The non-compact real form is realized at the
*infinitesimal / tangent* level as a Casimir-Hessian Lorentzian quadratic
form, even though it does not extend to a manifold-level Lie-group
action.

*Consequence.* Paper 14 Theorem 2.2′ remains the compact-real-form
selector for the classical gauge face on the cone; the §13.2 audit shows
the lift does not realize the non-compact real form *globally*; the
§13.2A audit shows the lift *does* realize it *locally* as a tangent-
sub-bundle quadratic form. Face (2) (nonabelian gauge, Paper 18
Theorem 6.1) stands at every level; face (1) (Lorentzian signature) is
refuted at the manifold level and confirmed at the local-tangent level.
The "two faces collapse to one ℂ²-leg face under a real-form choice"
reading is therefore correct at two layers — algebraic real-form duality
and local tangent-sub-bundle quadratic form — and incorrect at the
third (manifold-level Lie-group action).

### 9.4 The \(\hbar\)-role: the \(i\)-face's sibling invariant

Cor 9.7 Part 3 names the parallel; this subsection makes it explicit. The
imaginary-\(i\) face of Conjecture 9.4 (the complex-energy / leakage face;
Paper 20 §3) has its own invariant in exactly the sense that the signature
face has \(c\). The SLD/Bures metric of Paper 18 §4 is the *unique* monotone
metric on the lift whose conservative Nambu–Bures flow is unitary von Neumann
evolution \(\dot\rho=-i[H,\rho]\); under that selection the
Mandelstam–Tamm/Bures quantum speed limit specializes to
\[
\text{Bures distinguishability rate}\;=\;\frac{\Delta E}{\hbar},
\]
where \(\Delta E\) is the spread of the generator. The constant \(\hbar\) is
therefore the *conversion between the program's own statistical-distinguish-
ability clock and the unitary phase clock* — the \(i\)-face's slope exactly as
\(c\) is the signature-face's asymptote slope.

The pairing statement (the deliverable). The single Bures lift carries **two
paired conversion invariants, one per face**: \(c\) on the signature face
(space ↔ time), \(\hbar\) on the \(i\)-face (Fisher distinguishability clock ↔
unitary phase clock). Both are *derived to exist, derived to be invariant, and
structurally pinned*, while their *dimensionful magnitudes are permanently out
of scope* by §11.2 — identical epistemic status to \(c\equiv1\). The program
infers, of these constants, *that they exist, are invariant, and which face
each belongs to* — never their numerical values in any physical unit.

This adds no new audit: it reuses the Paper 18 §4 SLD/Bures selection (already
on the ledger as the Petz–Bures finite check, the same node that discharges
the general-covariance analog of §5.A and the H-theorem of the gravity
arc) and the Mandelstam–Tamm/Bures relation as an external anchor (peer-
reviewed precedent, not adopted ontology, per §11.1). The falsifier — if the
Bures distinguishability rate of the SLD/Bures flow is *not* proportional to
the generator energy spread with a single representation-independent
conversion constant, or if that constant is not lift-invariant in the way
\(c\) is — pre-claims the matched-pair negative branch: faces (3) and (4)
would remain four loose parallel faces of Corollary 9.7, not a paired
constant structure. Ledger item T′.

