### 9.8 The arrow and the Einstein-like equation are sign and magnitude of one object

**Corollary 9.11 (The arrow and the Einstein-like equation are the
sign and magnitude of one projection non-closure) [A]; \([P]\) hinge:
Paper 20 Remark 2.2; adds no new claim].**  *The projection-closure
residual — the jerk \(T^{(4)}=m^2_{\text{simplex}}\), the failure of
the projected back-path to preserve the closed-current condition
(Paper 20 Remark 1.1) — is a single object. Its sign is the arrow of
time (Corollary 9.9, proven definite by Paper 20 Remark 2.2's
\(m^2_{\text{simplex}}\ge 0\)); its magnitude is the gravity-adjacent
curvature defect (Corollary 9.10, sourcing [C] reduced, three GR
content-pillars inherited [P]). The arrow and the Einstein-like
equation are therefore not two phenomena but
\(\operatorname{sgn}(\cdot)\) and \(|\cdot|\) of one residual.*

**The sign disciplines the magnitude (the asymmetry that is the real
payoff).** The two readings sit at *different tiers*, and the proven
one constrains the conjectured one:

> Because the sign is **[P]**-fixed
> (\(m^2_{\text{simplex}}\ge 0\), Paper 20 Rmk 2.2), the
> projection-closure residual is **bounded-below, sign-definite,
> non-negative**. The R′ source is therefore necessarily non-negative:
> *the proven arrow is the program's positive-energy / weak-energy
> condition on the conjectured Einstein-like source.*

The arrow does not merely accompany R′; the proven sign disciplines
the conjectured magnitude — no negative source, no ghost, no
tachyonic LHS. This reproduces, as a **structural homolog (not a
derivation)**, the second-law ⟺ energy-condition shape that
underlies gravitational focusing / the area theorem. The program
exhibits the *form* of that linkage on one object — sign-definiteness
(arrow / 2nd law) ⟺ source non-negativity (WEC) — without claiming GR,
an area theorem, or thermodynamics.

**Tier and degradation (the corollary inherits, not asserts).**

- *The corollary statement is [A].* It asserts only that R′ and W′
  are the magnitude and sign of one object, plus the sign→magnitude
  constraint. It adds no new claim; it inherits the tiers of R′ and
  W′.
- *[P] core (survives everything):* the sign is definite
  (\(m^2_{\text{simplex}}\ge 0\)); hence a definite arrow exists *and*
  the source is non-negative, regardless of R′'s fate.
- *If R′ is falsified* (fixed-background cap): the corollary's
  *statement still stands* — the object's magnitude simply has no
  sourcing law; the magnitude↔sign decomposition is unaffected and the
  sign→magnitude non-negativity constraint still holds (it needed only
  the [P] sign).
- *If W′'s causal labelling is refuted* (Cor 9.9 fragile joint): the
  sign reading reverts to the bare [P] \(m^2\ge 0\) core, which still
  supplies the corollary's sign half and the sign→magnitude
  constraint. The unification is robust to W′'s most fragile sub-
  claim.

Every degradation path leaves the corollary *standing in a stated
weaker form*, never void.

**Falsifier (narrow, as befits an [A] consolidation).** The corollary
fails only if the R′ magnitude object and the W′ sign object are
*provably distinct objects* — i.e. the curvature-defect source and
the arrow-bearing residual are not \(|\cdot|\) and
\(\operatorname{sgn}(\cdot)\) of one projection-closure residual but
two structurally independent quantities. A single demonstration that
they are distinct refutes the unification (the corollary, not R′ or
W′ individually). Pre-claimed.

