## 10. The Two Answers, Closed

The session's opening questions, now answered with status tags:

- *Connection from information geometry / simplectic mechanics to physics.* **[P]**
  \(\Phi=\log Z\) is the categorical effective action (§3); physics splits its
  derivatives only because it presupposes a manifold; the program keeps them
  one tower.
- *What space and mass have in common.* **[P]** The second cumulant; space
  *is* inertia (§4).
- *What time, energy, and gravity have in common.* **[P]** The time-translation
  Noether generator and its closure failure: energy local current, gravity
  global holonomy (§5), localized arithmetically on \(\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\)
  to the conductor-6 carry (§6).
- *Why mass-structured beings experience 3D.* **[P]** It is the closure
  certificate of an inertial projector; two independent derivations select
  \(Q=4\), dimension 3 (§8); the non-spatial remainder is the gravity residual.
- *Why \(3+1\) and not just \(3\).* **[P] classical \((3,1)\)** from the
  Legendre \(-\ell_3\) sign (§9.1) as a contact-geometric / Legendre-dual
  split on the *fixed* Hellinger-sphere Riemannian background. **[SETTLED
  — two-level, §13.2 + §13.2A]:** the *manifold-level* Lorentzian promotion
  conjectured at §9.2 was refuted by §13.2 (framework is Euclidean-4
  globally); the *local-tangent* Lorentzian structure — a \((1,3)\) Casimir-
  Hessian sub-bundle of tangent vectors at \(\rho_*\) — is confirmed by
  §13.2A. The \((3,1)\) of Theorem 9.1 stands as a contact / Legendre-dual
  statement at the manifold level *and* matches the \(1+3\) split of the
  local Casimir sub-bundle, in agreement with the \(\mathbb H\) terminator
  decomposition of \(2\times 2\) Hermitians.
- *Why the speed of light is one / what \(c\) is.* **[P-adjacent]** \(c\equiv1\)
  is structurally *unity* — space and time are two derivatives of one
  \(\Phi\), so no independent normalization exists; this part stands and is
  unaffected by either audit. **[SETTLED — two-level, §13.2 + §13.2A]:**
  the *global* invariant-speed claim (asymptote of a manifold-level mass
  shell) is refuted by §13.2 (no global asymptote on a compact level set);
  the *local* invariant-speed claim (leading-order asymptotic slope of the
  Casimir hyperboloid at \(\rho_*\)) is confirmed by §13.2A — equal to \(1\)
  in internal Hilbert–Schmidt units when \(H\) and \(P_a\) are chosen with
  equal HS-norm. The dimensional firewall on the value of \(c\) in any
  physical unit (§11.2) is unchanged regardless.
- *Is this quantum gravity?* **[A, structural — added v0.2.5; ledger HH; §10.5; numerical content §10.6 / ledger II].**
  *In the structural-form sense, yes — at finite primorial, with gravity
  localized to the multiplicative-unit subgroup* \((\mathbb Z/P_k)^\times\)
  *of the conductor row (§2.5.4 / GG), with quantization realized as the
  Kummer cover at the cubic gauge* \(m=3\) *(T7 §5), with the gravity-matter
  decoupling forced by Pontryagin character orthogonality on the
  additive-vs-multiplicative partition of* \(\mathbb Z/N\) *(Paper 47 +
  §10.5.2). In the physical-Newtonian/Einsteinian/Planck sense, no — the
  §11.2 firewall is binding and no Newton constant, Planck scale, graviton
  mass, or metric on a 4-manifold is derived, claimed, or accessible. The
  framework realizes the **structural shape** of QG on a finite arithmetic
  substrate where the question is answerable; it does not derive the
  **physical content** of QG and is not in the same competitive lane as
  perturbative-QG / asymptotic-safety / AdS-CFT / loop-quantum-gravity /
  causal-dynamical-triangulations / string compactification. See §10.5 for
  the full Unification Thesis statement, §10.5.4 for the five-face
  refinement including the new Face E (Kummer-cover deck-group*
  \(\mathbb Z/2\) *at* \(N=6, m=3\)*), and §10.6 for the load-bearing
  numerical content* (\(\sin^2\theta_W|_{\text{tree}}=3/8\) *computed
  exactly, plus the dimensionless SM-skeleton predictions).*
- *Does the framework predict any dimensionless physical constant?* **[P, computed — added v0.2.5; ledger II; §10.6.1].**
  *Yes — the tree-level Weinberg angle*
  \(\sin^2\theta_W|_{\text{tree}}=3/8\). *The calculation is finite linear
  algebra on the 15-dim Connes–Chamseddine fermion representation*
  \(\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\) *with* \(E=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\)
  *forced by the CRT split at the smallest mixed primorial* \(\mathbb Z/6\)
  *and the prime-reciprocal hypercharge* \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\)
  *of Paper 33. All five Standard-Model hypercharge values reproduced exactly
  (§10.6.4). Generator-trace-squared values satisfy*
  \(\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(2)}^2)=\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(3)}^2)=2\), \(\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)=10/3\)
  *on the SM rep; tree value* \(\sin^2\theta_W=2/(2+10/3)=3/8\) *follows from
  GUT-normalization. The framework reproduces the SU(5) tree prediction
  without postulating SU(5) — it has* \(E=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\)
  *forced by finite arithmetic at* \(\mathbb Z/6\)*, and then*
