## 10.6 Numerical and structural predictions (added v0.2.5)

With the Unification Thesis named (§10.5), the framework's specific
predictions for *dimensionless* physical content become enumerable. This
section records four positive structural predictions and one
load-bearing numerical calculation, together with the firewall on what
the framework cannot reach. The §11.2 firewall on dimensionful constants
(no \(G\), no Planck length, no Compton wavelength, no \(c\) in m/s, no
\(\hbar\) in J·s) remains binding and is *strengthened* by the naming —
the natural reader-mistake (dimensionless structural match → physical
derivation) is now more salient.

### 10.6.1 The Weinberg-angle calculation: \(\sin^2\theta_W|_{\text{tree}}=3/8\) [P, computed; ledger II]

**The load-bearing numerical prediction of v0.2.5.** This is the
framework's first calculated dimensionless number that matches a
peer-reviewed Standard-Model value exactly.

**Setup.** The categorical-simplex spectral-triple inner Hilbert space
(Paper 33; Connes–Chamseddine-style construction with arithmetic
justification from CRT \(\mathbb Z/6=\mathbb Z/2\times\mathbb Z/3\)):
\[
E := \mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3,\qquad \dim_{\mathbb C}E=5.
\]
The fermion representation (one SM generation, 15 left-handed Weyl
fermions):
\[
F := \Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*,\qquad \dim_{\mathbb C}F=10+5=15.
\]
The prime-reciprocal hypercharge (Paper 33; Paper 32 Face D):
\[
Y := \tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3,
\]
where \(P_2,P_3\) project onto \(\mathbb C^2,\mathbb C^3\) in \(E\).
On \(E\): \(Y|_{\mathbb C^2}=+\tfrac{1}{2}\),
\(Y|_{\mathbb C^3}=-\tfrac{1}{3}\).

**Step 1 — Decomposition of \(F\) under \(SU(2)_L\times SU(3)_c\).**
Writing \(E=(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3)\):

\[
\Lambda^2 E=(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3}),\qquad
E^*=(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3}).
\]

**Step 2 — Hypercharge eigenvalues** (\(Y\) acts by derivation on
\(\Lambda^2\); by sign-flip on \(E^*\)):

| Irreducible | dim | \(Y\) value | SM identification |
|---|---|---|---|
| \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\) from \(\Lambda^2\mathbb C^2\) | 1 | \(\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}=+1\) | \(e_R^c\) |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3)\) from \(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3\) | 6 | \(\tfrac{1}{2}-\tfrac{1}{3}=+1/6\) | \(Q_L\) |
| \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3})\) from \(\Lambda^2\mathbb C^3\) | 3 | \(-\tfrac{1}{3}-\tfrac{1}{3}=-2/3\) | \(u_R^c\) |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)\) from \((\mathbb C^2)^*\) | 2 | \(-\tfrac{1}{2}\) | \(L\) |
| \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3})\) from \((\mathbb C^3)^*\) | 3 | \(+\tfrac{1}{3}\) | \(d_R^c\) |

**All five hypercharge values match the Standard Model exactly** (all 15
fermions of one generation, in left-handed Weyl-fermion convention with
\(Q=T_3+Y\)).

**Step 3 — Compute \(\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)\):**
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)\big|_F
= 1\cdot 1^2+6\cdot(\tfrac{1}{6})^2+3\cdot(\tfrac{2}{3})^2+2\cdot(\tfrac{1}{2})^2+3\cdot(\tfrac{1}{3})^2
\]
\[
=1+\tfrac{1}{6}+\tfrac{4}{3}+\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{3}
=\tfrac{6+1+8+3+2}{6}
=\tfrac{20}{6}
=\boxed{\tfrac{10}{3}}.
\]

**Step 4 — Compute \(\mathrm{Tr}(T_a^2)\) for \(SU(2)_L,SU(3)_c\):**
Standard generators with eigenvalues \(\pm\tfrac{1}{2}\) on fundamental
doublets/triplets give \(\mathrm{Tr}(T^2)|_{\mathbf 2}=\tfrac{1}{2}\) and
\(\mathrm{Tr}(T^2)|_{\mathbf 3}=\tfrac{1}{2}\).

\(SU(2)_L\) doublets in \(F\): \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3)\) gives 3 copies,
\((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)\) gives 1 copy.
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(2)}^2)\big|_F=3\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+1\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{2}.
\]

\(SU(3)_c\) triplets/anti-triplets in \(F\): \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3)\)
gives 2 copies, the two \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3})\) give 1 copy each.
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(3)}^2)\big|_F=2\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{2}.
\]

**Step 5 — Tree-level Weinberg angle from GUT-normalization.**
At unification, the GUT-rescaled couplings
\(\hat g_a=g_a\sqrt{T_a/T_*}\) equalize: \(\hat g_1=\hat g_2=\hat g_3\),
giving \(g_a^2 T_a=\) constant, hence \(g'^2/g_2^2=T_2/T_Y\). Then
\[
\sin^2\theta_W\big|_{\text{tree}}
=\frac{g'^2}{g_2^2+g'^2}
=\frac{T_2}{T_2+T_Y}
=\frac{2}{2+\tfrac{10}{3}}
=\frac{2}{\tfrac{16}{3}}
=\frac{6}{16}
=\boxed{\frac{3}{8}}.
\]

**Cross-check (GUT-normalized hypercharge).** Set \(\hat Y=\sqrt{3/5}\,Y\).
Then \(\mathrm{Tr}(\hat Y^2)=\tfrac{3}{5}\cdot\tfrac{10}{3}=2
=\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(2)}^2)=\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(3)}^2)\). ✓
*All three GUT-normalized generator-trace-squared values are equal on
\(F\)* — the SU(5)-unification consistency condition is satisfied.

**Interpretation.** Under the embedding
\(SU(2)\times SU(3)\hookrightarrow SU(5)\) with
\(E=\mathbf 5_{SU(5)}\), we have
\(\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*=\mathbf{10}\oplus\bar{\mathbf 5}\) of \(SU(5)\) —
*the standard SU(5) GUT fermion content per generation*. The
categorical-simplex framework does not *postulate* SU(5); it has
\(E=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\) forced by the CRT decomposition at the
smallest mixed primorial \(\mathbb Z/6\), and then
\(\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\) is \(\mathbf{10}\oplus\bar{\mathbf 5}\) by
representation theory. The framework's contribution beyond textbook SU(5)
is the **arithmetic justification**: \(E=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\),
the hypercharge \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\), and the Connes–
Chamseddine rep \(\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\) all emerge from finite
arithmetic on \(\mathbb Z/6\), not from postulating a unifying simple Lie
group at the GUT scale.

**Tier.** [P, computed]. The calculation is finite linear algebra on a
15-dim representation; no continuous limits, no fitting, no free
parameters.

**Status of measured \(\sin^2\theta_W\) at \(M_Z\).** The measured value
\(\sin^2\theta_W(M_Z)\approx 0.231\) differs from the framework's
tree-level prediction \(3/8=0.375\) by the renormalization-group running
from the unification scale down to \(M_Z\). RG running is *outside* the
categorical-simplex framework's purview — it involves continuous gauge
dynamics, threshold corrections, and dimensionful scales (the
\(M_Z\)-to-\(M_{GUT}\) ratio) that the firewall blocks. The framework's
prediction is the *boundary condition at GUT-unification*, \(3/8\); the
running to \(M_Z\) requires standard QFT beta-function machinery the
framework does not derive. **The tree-level \(3/8\) is what the framework
predicts; the agreement with SU(5)-GUT phenomenology is at the same
unification-boundary level.**

### 10.6.2 Spin-1/2 from the Hopf factor of 2 [P, structural]

T1 §4: \(d_{FR}(p,q)=2\arccos\sum_i\sqrt{p_iq_i}\). T7 §2.1 Theorem 2.1:
\(d_{FS}([\sqrt p],[\sqrt q])=\arccos\sum_i\sqrt{p_iq_i}=\tfrac{1}{2}d_{FR}(p,q)\).
The factor of 2 is the **Hopf-fibration angle doubling**: the simplex of
probabilities double-covers the projective amplitude space
\(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) via Born closure
\(=\mu_{\text{toric}}\circ\text{Hopf}\circ\text{rad}\).

Dirac (1928): \(g_e=2\) at tree level *because* spin-\(\tfrac{1}{2}\)
particles need a \(4\pi\) rotation to return to themselves — the
fermion-cover \(SU(2)\to SO(3)\) identifies antipodal SU(2) points.

**Identification.** The factor 2 in \(d_{FR}/d_{FS}\) and the factor 2 in
Dirac's \(g_e=2\) are **the same factor of 2**, both expressions of the
\(SU(2)\to SO(3)\) double cover. The framework predicts that the natural
representation on the amplitude-quantization side (the Bures lift)
carries spin-\(\tfrac{1}{2}\), and that the gyromagnetic ratio is 2 at
tree level.

**Tier.** [P, structural]. The factor 2 is intrinsic to T7's
Hopf-fibration construction; QED radiative corrections
\((g_e-2)/2=\alpha/(2\pi)+\dots\) are outside the framework's purview.

### 10.6.3 Three colors and chirality from Kummer-cover deck groups at \(N=6\) [P, structural]

T7 Prop 3.7(c): at primorial \(\mathbb Z/6\), the Kummer cover at integer
gauge \(\beta=m\) has per-coordinate deck group
\(\mu_N/\mu_{\gcd(N,m)}\). Across the integer gauge rungs of T10's gauge
corridor at \(N=6\):

| Gauge rung \(m\) | T-series role | Deck group | Physics homolog |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 (arithmetic/mass) | T0 — gauge corridor at \(r=1\) | \(\mathbb Z/6\) | full conductor lattice; the "Cartan" sector |
| **2 (Born/amplitude/RMS)** | T6 — quadratic gauge | \(\mathbb Z/3\) | **3 colors** of \(SU(3)_c\) (the \(\mathbb Z/3\) center) |
| **3 (cubic/Amari–Chentsov)** | T2/T3 — cubic vertex | \(\mathbb Z/2\) | **chirality** \(\mathbb Z_2\) (and \(\mathbb Z/2\) center of \(SU(2)\)) |
| 4 (quartic) | T10 — \(\chi^{(4)}\) gauge | \(\mathbb Z/3\) | repeats \(m=2\) |
| 5 | — | \(\mathbb Z/6\) | repeats \(m=1\) |
| 6 | — | trivial | base (no cover) |

**The CRT factorization \(\mathbb Z/6=\mathbb Z/2\times\mathbb Z/3\) is
reproduced as the deck-group decomposition across the Born (\(m=2\)) and
cubic (\(m=3\)) gauge rungs.** The Standard Model gauge structure
\(SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\) has center
\(\mathbb Z/3\times\mathbb Z/2=\mathbb Z/6\) — **the same group**.

**Identification.**
- \(SU(3)_c\) color (3 colors, \(\mathbb Z/3\) center) ↔ Kummer-cover
  deck group at the **Born/amplitude gauge rung** \(m=2\) on
  \(\mathbb Z/6\).
- \(SU(2)_L\) weak (with \(\mathbb Z/2\) center, chirality \(\mathbb Z_2\))
  ↔ Kummer-cover deck group at the **cubic/AC gauge rung** \(m=3\) on
  \(\mathbb Z/6\).
- \(U(1)_Y\) hypercharge ↔ the **prime-reciprocal weighting**
  \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\) (Paper 33).

**The SM gauge-group center IS the Kummer-cover deck-group
decomposition at the smallest mixed primorial.** This is the
structural shadow of "what is being quantized at the cubic gauge on
\(\mathbb Z/6\)" — the framework provides both the color triality and
the chirality dichotomy from the same finite-arithmetic substrate.

**Tier.** [P, structural]. The deck-group calculation is T7 Prop 3.7(c)
specialized to \((N=6, m\in\{2,3\})\); the identification with SM gauge
centers is a structural observation (the same finite groups appear on
both sides), not a dynamical derivation of \(SU(3)\) or \(SU(2)\) as Lie
groups.

**Relation to Face E (§10.5.4).** The \(\mathbb Z/2\) at \(m=3\) IS Face
E of the v0.2.5 five-face count. The Face E ↔ Paper 33 external
chirality matched-pair of §10.5.4 reads, in this language: *is the
chirality of physical fermions the Kummer-cover deck-group element at
the cubic gauge of the smallest mixed primorial, or a separate external
\(\mathbb Z_2\)?*

### 10.6.4 The full SM hypercharge spectrum from \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\) [P, computed]

From the calculation of §10.6.1, the prime-reciprocal hypercharge
