**Tier.** [P, computed]. Five for five, no free parameters, no fitting.

### 10.6.5 \(3+1\) dimensions [P, inherited from §8, §9]

Already established in Paper 32 §8 (dimensional selection) and §9.1
(classical signature \((3,1)\) from Legendre \(-\ell_3\) sign). The
T-series substrate adds two reinforcements (§2.5.2):

- T1 §6–7: the tetrahedral case \(N=4\) has \(H_0\) of dimension 3, with
  intrinsic vector algebra (cross product, Rodrigues rotation) that *is*
  ordinary 3D Euclidean vector calculus exactly.
- T7 §1: the Hopf+toric fibration provides the canonical lift from
  \(\Delta^{N-1}\) to \(S^{2N-1}\to\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\), with the
  decoherent face being 3-dimensional at \(N=4\).

The closure-valid block \(A_2\oplus A_3\) at \(\mathbb Z/6\) has
dimension 3 (Paper 32 §6.1), inheriting the same 3D structure within
\(H_6\). The signature \((1,3,4)\) on the local Casimir at \(\rho_*\)
(Paper 32 §13.2A, Paper 46) gives 3+1 spacetime at the local-tangent
level.

**Tier.** [P, structural]. Multiple independent routes (Paper 32 Hodge,
Paper 32 conductor count, T1 tetrahedral 3D algebra) converge on the
same conclusion.

### 10.6.6 What the framework cannot reach (firewall summary)

The §11.2 firewall on dimensionful constants remains binding. The
following are **out of scope** by construction:

| Target | Why unreachable |
|---|---|
| \(\alpha\approx 1/137.036\) | 137 is prime but at no natural framework row; framework has no charge primitive |
| \(m_p/m_e\approx 1836\) | requires QCD confinement scale, fermion mass primitives |
| \(\alpha_s(M_Z)\approx 0.118\) | requires running coupling and confinement scale |
| Measured \(\sin^2\theta_W(M_Z)\approx 0.231\) | requires RG running from GUT scale; framework only predicts tree value \(3/8\) |
| CKM phases, neutrino masses | no flavor primitive |
| Cosmological \(\Lambda\) in \(eV^4\) | dimensionful, firewall-binding |
| Hubble \(H_0\) in s\(^{-1}\) | dimensionful |
| Newton constant \(G\), Planck scale, graviton mass | dimensionful, ledger R′ firewall |
| Number of generations | not derived (the framework gives one generation per copy of \(F\)) |
| Specific masses (Higgs, electron, top) | dimensionful, no mass primitive |

The framework predicts the **dimensionless skeleton of the Standard
Model** — gauge structure, chirality, hypercharge quantization, the
tree-level Weinberg angle, spin-1/2, 3+1 dimensions — from finite
arithmetic on the smallest mixed primorial \(\mathbb Z/6\). It does
**not** predict the **dimensionful magnitudes** that running, masses,
mixing angles, and cosmological evolution require. The firewall stays
binding precisely on the magnitudes.

### 10.6.7a Fermion-boson selection rule: spin-statistics on the categorical simplex [P, structural; partial computational; ledger JJ]

**Three \(\mathbb Z/2\)'s converge at the smallest mixed primorial.** The
framework's spin-statistics correspondence assembles three different
\(\mathbb Z/2\)'s that coincide at \((N=6, m=3)\):

**(F-i) Hopf-fibration parity.** T7 §2.1 Theorem 2.1: a \(2\pi\) rotation
of the projective base \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) lifts to multiplication by
\(-1\) on the amplitude \(z\in S^{2N-1}\). Spinor sections of the
non-trivial Hopf line bundle pick up \(-1\); scalar sections (trivial
bundle) do not. *This is the spin half: \((-1)^{2s}\) on integer- vs
half-integer-spin fields.*

**(F-ii) Kummer-cover deck-element.** T7 Prop 3.7(c) at
\((N=6, m=3)\): per-coordinate deck group is
\(\mu_6/\mu_3\cong\mathbb Z/2\), generated by
\(\xi: z\mapsto -z\). *This is the cubic-gauge parity (Face E of
§10.5.4).*

**(F-iii) Tensor-exchange parity.** On \(E\otimes E\), the swap operator
has eigenvalues \(+1\) on \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) and \(-1\) on
\(\Lambda^2 E\). *This is the statistics half: antisymmetric vs
symmetric exchange.*

**Calculation: deck action on tensor modules.** \(\xi\) acts on
\(E=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\) by \(e\mapsto -e\). On
\(E^{\otimes k}\), the diagonal deck gives \(\xi^{\otimes k}=(-1)^k\):

| Module | dim | Diagonal deck \(\xi^{\otimes k}\) | Exchange \(\sigma\) |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(E\) | 5 | \(-1\) | (degree 1, n/a) |
| \(E^*\) | 5 | \(-1\) | (degree 1, n/a) |
| \(\Lambda^2 E\) | 10 | \(+1\) | \(-1\) |
| \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) | 15 | \(+1\) | \(+1\) |

**Puzzle.** The diagonal deck does *not* distinguish \(\Lambda^2 E\) from
\(\text{Sym}^2 E\) — both have \(\xi^{\otimes 2}=+1\). The deck alone is
not the fermion-boson selector at the tensor-product level.

**Resolution: single-spinor parity.** The fermion-parity \(\mathbb Z/2\)
acts on the *individual* spinor \(z\in S^{2N-1}\), not on the diagonal
of \(E^{\otimes k}\). Each basis vector \(e_i\in\mathbb C^2\subset E\)
is a section of the Hopf bundle over \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\). Under a
\(2\pi\) rotation in the projective base, that section picks up \(-1\)
— the Dirac sign. **The Hopf-bundle parity (F-i) and the Kummer-cover
deck-element (F-ii) coincide as the \(\mathbb Z/2\) that flips the sign
of a single spinor under "\(2\pi\) rotation seen from the amplitude
side."**

**Two-particle exchange.** Take two spinor fields \(\psi_1,\psi_2\) and
form the two-particle state. For Slater-determinant (antisymmetric)
state in \(\Lambda^2 E\):
\[
\psi_1(z_1)\psi_2(z_2)-\psi_2(z_1)\psi_1(z_2)
\xrightarrow{z_1\leftrightarrow z_2}
-\bigl[\psi_1(z_1)\psi_2(z_2)-\psi_2(z_1)\psi_1(z_2)\bigr]
\]
\(\Lambda^2 E\) states pick up \(-1\) under exchange — fermionic
statistics by construction.

For a symmetric state in \(\text{Sym}^2 E\): pickup is \(+1\) — bosonic
statistics.

**The three \(\mathbb Z/2\)'s align.** At \((N=6, m=3)\):
\[
\underbrace{(-1)^{2s}}_{\text{Hopf parity (spin)}}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\xi\big|_{\text{individual spinor}}}_{\text{Kummer deck at }(N=6,m=3)}
\;=\;
\underbrace{\sigma\big|_{E\otimes E}}_{\text{exchange parity (statistics)}}
\]
Each factor of \(E\) in the fermion module \(F=\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\)
is a single spinor (Hopf parity \(-1\), Kummer deck \(-1\)). The
antisymmetric pairing \(\Lambda^2\) gives exchange parity \(-1\). **All
three \(-1\)'s describe the same physical fact: spin-\(\tfrac{1}{2}\)
fermions exchange antisymmetrically.**

**Bosonic-counterpart module \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) (computational).** For
comparison with the fermion calculation of §10.6.1, decompose
\(\text{Sym}^2 E\) and compute its hypercharge trace:

| Irreducible | dim | \(Y\) | Comparable SM boson? |
|---|---|---|---|
| \(\text{Sym}^2(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)=(\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\) | 3 | \(+1\) | none (\(W\)-triplet has \(Y=0\)) |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1)\otimes(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3)=(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3)\) | 6 | \(+1/6\) | none (would be a "bosonic \(Q_L\)") |
| \(\text{Sym}^2(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3)=(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 6)\) | 6 | \(-2/3\) | none (gluons in adjoint \(\mathbf 8\), not \(\mathbf 6\)) |

Total \(\dim=15=\binom{6}{2}\) ✓. Trace:
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)\big|_{\text{Sym}^2 E}
=3\cdot 1^2+6\cdot(\tfrac{1}{6})^2+6\cdot(\tfrac{2}{3})^2
=3+\tfrac{1}{6}+\tfrac{8}{3}=\boxed{\tfrac{35}{6}}.
\]

**Result.** \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) is bosonic (symmetric, \(\sigma=+1\)) but
does *not* match SM bosonic content. The SM gauge bosons live in
\(\text{End}(E)\) = adjoint
\(\mathfrak{su}(2)\oplus\mathfrak{su}(3)\oplus\mathfrak{u}(1)_Y\)
(\(\dim 3+8+1=12\)); the SM Higgs lives in a separate bimodule.

**The framework's \(\Lambda\)/\(\text{Sym}\) dichotomy gives the
spin-statistics skeleton (fermion=antisymmetric=\(\Lambda\),
boson=symmetric=\(\text{Sym}\)), but SM bosonic content requires
additional construction beyond \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) alone.**

**Selection rule (theorem statement).** *A field \(\Psi\) on the
categorical-simplex Bures lift is **fermionic** iff:*
(1) *it is a section of the non-trivial Hopf line bundle over*
    \(\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) *(picks up* \(-1\) *under* \(2\pi\) *rotation
    in the projective base, equivalently the Kummer-cover deck-element
    at* \((N=6, m=3)\)*);*
(2) *it appears as an antisymmetric tensor (in* \(\Lambda^k E\) *for
    some* \(k\)*, equivalently the Connes–Chamseddine module*
    \(F=\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\)*).*

*At \((N=6, m=3)\) these two conditions are **equivalent**: the
Hopf-bundle parity equals the Kummer-cover deck-element, and \(F\) is
the unique 15-dim antisymmetric module whose fermion content matches
one SM generation exactly (§10.6.1).*

*A field is **bosonic** iff both parities are \(+1\): a section of the
trivial Hopf line bundle, appearing in a symmetric or endomorphism
module.*

**Tier.** [P, structural; partial computational]. The fermion side
(\(F = \Lambda^2 E \oplus E^*\) at \(\xi = -1\), antisymmetric,
spin-\(\tfrac{1}{2}\)) is computed exactly at §10.6.1 / §10.6.4. The
bosonic side (\(\text{Sym}^2 E\) at \(\xi = +1\), symmetric, spin-0) is
presented here as the symmetric-tensor counterpart at [P, computed]
but does not match SM bosonic content directly. The three-\(\mathbb
Z/2\)-alignment is [A, structural].

**What the framework derives:**
- Fermions go in \(\Lambda\); bosons go in \(\text{Sym}\) (structural)
- Spin-\(\tfrac{1}{2}\) ↔ Hopf-bundle non-trivial section (structural,
  via factor of 2)
- The three \(\mathbb Z/2\)'s coincide at \((N=6, m=3)\) (structural)

**What the framework does NOT derive:**
- The standard QFT proof of spin-statistics from Lorentz invariance +
  microcausality (the framework is finite-arithmetic, not
  Lorentz-invariant at the continuous level globally; only the
  local-Casimir Lorentzian sub-bundle at \(\rho_*\) per §13.2A)
- The specific SM bosonic content (gauge bosons in adjoint, Higgs in
  bimodule) — requires additional spectral-triple construction beyond
  \(\text{Sym}^2 E\) alone
- The QFT operator-algebra anticommutation relations
  \(\{\psi_a,\psi_b^\dagger\}=\delta_{ab}\) — the framework provides
  the structural skeleton but not the field-quantization
  anticommutators directly

### 10.6.7b Going to \(\mathbb Z/30\): the GUT-scale row [P, computed; ledger KK]

The natural question after the Z/6 calculation of §10.6.1: what does going to
the next squarefree primorial \(\mathbb Z/30=\mathbb Z/(2\cdot 3\cdot 5)\)
predict? The answer is computable and is itself a structural test of the
framework: if Z/30 reproduces SM at low energy, the framework is in trouble
(no observed SU(5) gauge factor); if it reproduces SU(5)-GUT structure at
the GUT scale, the framework predicts the SM↔GUT transition naturally.

**Setup (parallel to §10.6.1).** CRT: \(\mathbb Z/30=\mathbb Z/2\times\mathbb
Z/3\times\mathbb Z/5\). Spectral-triple inner Hilbert space:
\[
E_{30}:=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3\oplus\mathbb C^5,\qquad\dim E_{30}=10.
\]
Fermion module:
\[
F_{30}:=\Lambda^2 E_{30}\oplus E_{30}^*,\qquad\dim F_{30}=45+10=55.
\]
Hypercharge unchanged from Z/6: \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\), with
\(P_5\) acting as 0 (no new prime-reciprocal term for the fifth prime at
this rung; \(Y\) restricted to \(E_{30}\) eigenvalues
\((1/2, -1/3, 0)\) per component).

**Step 1 — Decomposition of \(F_{30}\) under \(SU(2)\times SU(3)\times SU(5)\).**
Writing \(E_{30}=(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)\):

\[
\Lambda^2 E_{30}=(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf{10})\oplus(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 5),
\]
\[
E_{30}^*=(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)\oplus(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 5}).
\]

**\(F_{30}\) hypercharge content:**

| Irrep | dim | \(Y\) | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\) | 1 | \(+1\) | \(\Lambda^2\mathbb C^2\) |
| \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)\) | 3 | \(-2/3\) | \(\Lambda^2\mathbb C^3\) |
| **\((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf{10})\)** | **10** | **0** | **\(\Lambda^2\mathbb C^5\): the SU(5) decuplet** |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\) | 6 | \(+1/6\) | \(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3\) (the Z/6 \(Q_L\)) |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)\) | 10 | \(+1/2\) | \(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^5\) |
| \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 5)\) | 15 | \(-1/3\) | \(\mathbb C^3\otimes\mathbb C^5\) |
| \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\) | 2 | \(-1/2\) | \((\mathbb C^2)^*\) (the Z/6 \(L\)) |
| \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)\) | 3 | \(+1/3\) | \((\mathbb C^3)^*\) (the Z/6 \(d_R^c\)) |
| **\((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 5})\)** | **5** | **0** | **\((\mathbb C^5)^*\): the SU(5) antifundamental** |

Total \(\dim F_{30}=55\), **not** \(3\times 15=45\) of three SM generations.

**Step 2 — Trace calculations.**

\[
\mathrm{Tr}(Y^2)\big|_{F_{30}}=1+\tfrac{4}{3}+0+\tfrac{1}{6}+\tfrac{5}{2}+\tfrac{5}{3}+\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{3}+0=\tfrac{45}{6}=\boxed{\tfrac{15}{2}}
\]

\(SU(2)\) doublets: \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\) ×3 (color), \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)\) ×5 (SU(5)-fund), \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)\) ×1.
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(2)}^2)\big|_{F_{30}}=3\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+5\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+1\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{\tfrac{9}{2}}.
\]

\(SU(3)\) triplets/antitriplets: \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)_\Lambda\), \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)\) ×2 doublet, \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 5)\) ×5, \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)_*\).
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(3)}^2)\big|_{F_{30}}=\tfrac{1}{2}+2\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+5\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}=\boxed{\tfrac{9}{2}}.
\]

\(SU(5)\) (using \(\mathrm{Tr}(T^2)|_{\mathbf 5}=1/2\), \(\mathrm{Tr}(T^2)|_{\mathbf{10}}=3/2\)):
\[
\mathrm{Tr}(T_{SU(5)}^2)\big|_{F_{30}}=\underbrace{\tfrac{3}{2}}_{\mathbf{10}}+\underbrace{2\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}}_{(\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)}+\underbrace{3\cdot\tfrac{1}{2}}_{(\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 5)}+\underbrace{\tfrac{1}{2}}_{\bar{\mathbf 5}}=\boxed{\tfrac{9}{2}}.
\]

**Three-way trace coincidence:** \(T_{SU(2)}=T_{SU(3)}=T_{SU(5)}=9/2\) on \(F_{30}\) — *the categorical-simplex three-gauge unification condition is satisfied automatically*.

**Step 3 — Tree-level Weinberg-like angles.**
For each gauge factor \(a\in\{2,3,5\}\):
\[
\sin^2\theta^{(a)}\big|_{\text{tree}}=\frac{T_a}{T_a+T_Y}=\frac{9/2}{(9/2)+(15/2)}=\frac{9}{24}=\boxed{\frac{3}{8}}.
\]

**The same 3/8 as Z/6 (§10.6.1), now for all three gauge factors** against U(1)\_Y. The prime-reciprocal hypercharge structure preserves \(\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}=3/8\) across the primorial extension.

**Step 4 — Kummer-cover deck groups at Z/30.**
T7 Prop 3.7(c) gives per-coordinate deck \(\mu_{30}/\mu_{\gcd(30,m)}\):

| \(m\) | Deck group | Physics at Z/30 |
|---|---|---|
| 1 (mass) | \(\mathbb Z/30\) | full conductor |
| 2 (Born) | \(\mathbb Z/15\) | refined 15-fold, **NOT** 3 colors |
| 3 (cubic) | \(\mathbb Z/10\) | refined 10-fold, **NOT** 2 chiralities |
| **5** | \(\mathbb Z/6\) | **the SM gauge center \(\mathbb Z/6=\mathbb Z/3\times\mathbb Z/2\) appears here** |
| 6 | \(\mathbb Z/5\) | five-fold (new \(\mathbb Z/5\)) |
| **10** | \(\mathbb Z/3\) | **three colors emerge** |
| **15** | \(\mathbb Z/2\) | **chirality emerges** |
| 30 | trivial | base |

**At Z/30, the SM gauge-group center \(\mathbb Z/6\) appears at the *quintic* gauge rung \(m=5\)** — not at Born (\(m=2\)) or cubic (\(m=3\)) as at Z/6. Color and chirality individually emerge at \(m=10\) and \(m=15\) respectively.

**Step 5 — Interpretation: Z/30 as the GUT row.**

The framework's content at Z/30:

1. The fermion module \(F_{30}\) contains the **SU(5) decuplet \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf{10})\) and antifundamental \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 5})\)** — together totaling 15 dim, precisely *one generation's worth of SU(5)-GUT fermion content* embedded in the categorical-simplex framework as the \(\mathbb C^5\)-factor's contribution.
2. **Three-way trace coincidence \(T_2=T_3=T_5=9/2\)** is the categorical-simplex three-gauge unification condition — automatically satisfied, not imposed.
3. **\(\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}=3/8\) preserved** across the Z/6 → Z/30 extension for all three gauge-vs-hypercharge angles.
4. Hypercharge quantization unit remains \(1/N\) with \(N=30\) (refined from \(1/6\)); SM hypercharge values are still embedded as the Z/6-substructure (the \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 1)_{Y=1/6}\) and \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1)_{Y=-1/2}\) and \((\mathbf 1,\bar{\mathbf 3},\mathbf 1)_{Y=1/3}\) sectors from \(\mathbb C^2 \oplus \mathbb C^3\)).
5. SU(5)-only sectors (the decuplet, the antifundamental, the \((\mathbf 2,\mathbf 1,\mathbf 5)\), \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 3,\mathbf 5)\)) carry **hypercharges \((+1/2, 0, -1/3, 0)\) on the \(\mathbb C^5\) part**, none of which match SM "fermion-content" exactly — these are extra particles at the Z/30 row.

**The natural interpretation:** *Z/30 is the GUT-scale row of the categorical-simplex framework.* SU(5) appears as a third gauge factor at the GUT scale; the Z/6 ↔ Z/30 transition is the framework's structural shadow of the SM ↔ GUT transition. The SM gauge content (SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1) at center \(\mathbb Z/6\)) is *visible at Z/30 only at the quintic gauge rung* \(m=5\), not at the natural Born/cubic gauges — exactly the structural signature of "SM is the low-energy effective theory; Z/30 is the high-energy GUT row."

### Step 6 — What Z/30 predicts and what it disfavors

**Z/30 predicts (positive):**

- **GUT-scale SU(5)-shaped structure** with \(\sin^2\theta^{(a)}_{\text{tree}}=3/8\) for all three gauge-vs-hypercharge angles.
- **Automatic three-gauge unification** at the framework's natural normalization: \(T_2=T_3=T_5=9/2\) on \(F_{30}\).
- **SU(5) decuplet \((\mathbf 1,\mathbf 1,\mathbf{10})\) and antifundamental \(\bar{\mathbf 5}\)** as new fermion sectors at GUT scale — *the categorical-simplex framework names the SU(5)-GUT 15-fermion-per-generation embedding directly via the \(\mathbb C^5\)-leg of the spectral triple at Z/30*.
- **Hypercharge quantization refines from \(1/6\) (Z/6) to \(1/30\) (Z/30)** — the GUT-scale finer hypercharge unit.
- **Z/6 SM gauge-center \(\mathbb Z/6\) appears at the quintic gauge \(m=5\) on Z/30** — the structural signature of SM-content embedded in Z/30 at a specific (non-natural-Born/cubic) gauge rung.

**Z/30 disfavors (falsifies):**

- **"Primes in the row = generations":** \(F_{30}\) has dim 55, not \(3\times 15=45\) of three SM generations. Generation count is NOT directly determined by the prime count of the primorial row. The framework's prediction of three generations needs an independent mechanism beyond "we live at Z/30."
- **"Z/30 IS the SM row":** SU(5) gauge factor is not in SM, and the natural Born/cubic gauges give \(\mathbb Z/15\) and \(\mathbb Z/10\) deck groups (not SM's \(\mathbb Z/3, \mathbb Z/2\)). So the SM does not live at Z/30 directly.
- **Naive primorial extension to Z/210:** \(\mathbb Z/210=\mathbb Z/(2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdot 7)\) would predict a 4-gauge-factor structure SU(2)×SU(3)×SU(5)×SU(7). No SU(7) gauge factor is observed at any energy (the standard GUT-unification scheme uses SU(5) or SO(10), not SU(7)). So Z/210 likely lies *above* the GUT scale, in a regime no current physics probes.

**Z/30 confirms (cross-validates Z/6 work):**

- \(\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}=3/8\) is **stable** across the Z/6 → Z/30 extension. Not a coincidence at Z/6.
- The \(\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\) construction is **scale-invariant** — it gives the right SU(5)-GUT-shape structure at Z/30 just as it gave the right SM-generation-shape structure at Z/6.
- The prime-reciprocal hypercharge \(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\) **does not require modification** at Z/30 — the new prime 5 contributes hypercharge 0, making the Z/6 hypercharge structure persist through to the GUT-scale extension.

**Tier.** [P, computed]. All trace calculations are finite linear algebra on the 55-dim \(F_{30}\); no continuous limits, no fitting, no free parameters.

**Status of "is this real GUT physics?"** The framework reproduces the *structural form* of SU(5) GUT phenomenology — the right tree Weinberg angle, the right fermion content of one SU(5) generation as a sub-sector, the right three-gauge trace unification. It does **not** derive the GUT scale itself (a dimensionful quantity, firewall-binding), the running of couplings, threshold corrections, proton decay rate, or any dimensionful GUT-scale prediction. The framework provides the SU(5)-GUT *skeleton* on the categorical-simplex substrate; the physical GUT content remains outside scope.

### 10.6.7c Going to \(\mathbb Z/12\) and \(\mathbb Z/18\): the depth rows [P, computed; ledger LL]

After Z/30 = SU(5)-GUT extension (§10.6.7b), the natural further question:
what do the **non-squarefree** rows \(\mathbb Z/12=\mathbb Z/(2^2\cdot 3)\)
and \(\mathbb Z/18=\mathbb Z/(2\cdot 3^2)\) predict? These rows are
explicitly used in Paper 32 §13.1 as the depth-audit of the [A] items
(prime-power persistence of the conductor-6 residual); the framework's
behavior here is already partially settled (Paper 47 §8.4 audit χ K-branch
on the binary V/M, with V/W/M trichotomy joint-on-disjoint at the tangent
tier). This subsection completes the prediction.

**Conductor packet structure.**

*\(\mathbb Z/12 = \mathbb Z/4\times\mathbb Z/3\)* (CRT, \(\gcd(4,3)=1\));
divisors \(>1\): \(\{2,3,4,6,12\}\):

| \(d\) | \(\varphi(d)\) | Type |
|---|---|---|
| 2 | 1 | single-prime (parity) |
| 3 | 2 | single-prime (cube-root) |
| **4** | **2** | **prime-power-2-depth (NEW vs Z/6)** |
| 6 | 2 | cross-prime |
| 12 | 4 | full conductor \(=(\mathbb Z/12)^\times\) |

Sum: \(1+2+2+2+4 = 11 = N-1\) ✓.

*\(\mathbb Z/18 = \mathbb Z/2\times\mathbb Z/9\)* (CRT); divisors \(>1\):
\(\{2,3,6,9,18\}\):

| \(d\) | \(\varphi(d)\) | Type |
|---|---|---|
| 2 | 1 | single-prime (parity) |
| 3 | 2 | single-prime (cube-root) |
| 6 | 2 | cross-prime |
| **9** | **6** | **prime-power-3-depth (NEW vs Z/6)** |
| 18 | 6 | full conductor \(=(\mathbb Z/18)^\times\) |

Sum: \(1+2+2+6+6 = 17 = N-1\) ✓.

**Step 1 — Spectral-triple Hilbert space is unchanged from Z/6.**
Both \(\mathbb Z/12\) and \(\mathbb Z/18\) have the same prime divisors as
\(\mathbb Z/6\) (namely 2 and 3). The framework's spectral-triple inner
Hilbert space tracks *primes*, not *prime powers*:
\[
E_{12}=E_{18}=\mathbb C^2\oplus\mathbb C^3=E_6,\qquad\dim=5.
\]
Consequence: the fermion module \(F=\Lambda^2 E\oplus E^*\) is identical to
the Z/6 case — dim 15, one SM generation, with hypercharges
\(Y=\tfrac{1}{2}P_2-\tfrac{1}{3}P_3\) unchanged.

\[
\boxed{\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}\big|_{\mathbb Z/12}=\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}\big|_{\mathbb Z/18}=\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}\big|_{\mathbb Z/6}=\frac{3}{8}}
\]

**The framework predicts \(\sin^2\theta_W^{\text{tree}}=3/8\) is *invariant
under prime-power depth*.** Depth changes the conductor packet structure
but not the prime structure, and the spectral-triple Weinberg-angle
calculation depends only on the primes.

**Step 2 — Kummer-cover deck groups at integer gauges (the new structure).**
T7 Prop 3.7(c) gives per-coordinate deck \(\mu_N/\mu_{\gcd(N,m)}\):

