## 13. The Two Instrumented Audits

### 13.1 Depth audit (settles [A] items 8, B-bound, S-sign)

Reuse Paper 17 §5's exact \(\mathbb{Z}/6\hookrightarrow\mathbb{Z}/12,
\mathbb{Z}/18\) Constraint-3 procedure on the 5-dimensional exact integrator of
Paper 14, instrumented to read off \(\|R^{\Pi_\tau}\|\) on the conductor-6
mode specifically and the sign of the conductor-6 Noether form. Pass condition:
the residual remains supported on the squarefree conductor-6 mode, bounded, and
sign-definite under prime-power depth (\(2^2\), \(3^2\)). Fail ⇒ a new
depth-induced carry axis, refuting time-translation closure on the row and
flagging a Paper 22 stress point. This is Figure 1; the full pre-registered
run specification, observables, controls, panel layout, and verdict
inequalities are given in **Appendix F** (F.5 is the depth overlay).

### 13.2 Bures-dispersion audit — SETTLED: Euclidean-4 (item L)

**Audit specification (verbatim, the pre-registered question).** Lift the
energy to \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\) on \(D(\mathbb C^2\otimes\mathbb C^3)\)
(Paper 18), restricted to the conductor-2 and conductor-3 packets. Check
(i) the level set \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)=E\) is a genuine two-sheeted
hyperboloid, and (ii) it is invariant under a 3-parameter group completing the
Cartan flow. Outcome (i)+(ii) ⇒ Lorentzian \(3+1\) derived as a lift
phenomenon (item K confirmed). Outcome "ellipsoid / wrong-dimensional group"
⇒ intrinsically Euclidean-4 (item L confirmed). Both outcomes are publishable
results pre-claimed in §12. Free read-off: the asymptotic slope of the same
level set is the invariant speed of Corollary 9.7; a genuine hyperboloid
confirms a finite invariant speed (item J′), while a definite level set
confirms there is none.

**Settlement.** The audit has been run (script
`empirical/paper32_13_2_bures_dispersion_audit.py`, deterministic transcript
`empirical/paper32_13_2_audit_run_output.txt`) on the sub-Hilbert-space
\(V=A_2\oplus A_3\subset\mathbb C^6\) (character basis \(\chi_3,\chi_2,\chi_4\),
\(\dim_{\mathbb C}V=3\), \(\dim_{\mathbb R}D(V)=8\)) across five distinct
Cartan-respecting Hamiltonians \(H=a(\sigma_3\!\otimes\! I)+b(I\!\otimes\!
\lambda_3)+c(I\!\otimes\!\lambda_8)\) covering single-Cartan and
combined-Cartan cases. Four diagnostic tests, with the same verdict on all
five runs:

- **(A) Signature of \(H_V\).** Generic: \((1,1,1)\) — indefinite. This
  passes the necessary condition for a hyperboloid in the purification
  picture (positive and negative eigenvalues both present).
- **(B) Topology of the level set.** \(\{\rho\in D(V):\operatorname{tr}
  (H_V\rho)=E\}\) is the intersection of a hyperplane with the convex compact
  set \(D(V)\); for \(E\) strictly inside the spectrum it is a convex compact
  subset of real dimension \(7\). **Connected. Compact. No two-sheeted
  decomposition. No non-compact asymptote.**
- **(C) Bures metric signature at \(\rho_*=I/3\).** At the centroid the Bures
  metric reduces to \(3\cdot I_8\) on the orthonormal Gell-Mann tangent basis
  — signature \((8,0,0)\), positive-definite Riemannian. *Not*
  pseudo-Riemannian; no \((1,n)\) signature for a hyperboloid embedding.
- **(D) Cartan-completing symmetry group.** For non-degenerate \(H_V\) the
  centralizer in \(U(3)\) is the maximal torus \(U(1)^3\) — matches the
  conjecture's "3-parameter group" count, but **compact and toric**, not the
  non-compact \(\mathrm{SO}(3,1)/\mathrm{SO}(3)\) boost group required for a
  Lorentzian level-set action. \(U(3)\) is compact; *every* subgroup is
  compact.

Test (A) admits the algebraic possibility of a hyperboloid-shaped quadratic
form on the purified states; tests (B), (C), (D) all return the Euclidean-4
branch decisively. Robust to the choice of Cartan-respecting \(H\): five
distinct runs, same verdict.

**Verdict.**
\[
\boxed{\;\text{Item } K \text{ REFUTED;}\quad \text{Item } L \text{ CONFIRMED;}\quad
\text{Item } J' \text{ falls.}\;}
\]
The matched-pair branch L (intrinsically Euclidean-4) was pre-claimed in §12
as an equally publishable outcome of this audit; it is now the realized one.
Item J′ (finite invariant speed \(c\) as the hyperboloid asymptote) falls
with K: there is no asymptote on a compact level set.

**Net effect on Corollary 9.7 ("one lift, four faces").** The faces partition
into refuted and untouched:
- face (1) Lorentzian signature → refuted on the conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3
  row;
- face (2) nonabelian gauge restoration → untouched (Paper 18 Theorem 6.1
  is independent of dispersion);
- face (3) imaginary \(i\) / invariant \(\hbar\) → untouched (ledger item
  T′; Mandelstam–Tamm/Bures is a statement about distinguishability *rate*
  vs energy spread, independent of whether the energy *level set* is a
  hyperboloid);
- face (4) invariant speed \(c\) → refuted (item J′ falls with K).

Theorem 9.1's classical \((3,1)\) signature stands as a contact-geometric /
Legendre-dual split of the Poincaré–Cartan one-form on the *fixed* Hellinger-
sphere Riemannian background (§9.1, Remark 9.1b); it is *not* promoted to a
Lorentzian metric by the Bures lift on this row. Lemma 9.2's "Galilean, not
Lorentzian" disclaimer is *not* lifted.

**Untouched downstream.** Items R′ (Einstein-like equation magnitude), W′
(arrow / DPI), X′ (magnitude↔sign), Z′ (Raychaudhuri focusing-scalar
localization), the four-thread Corollary 6.3, and the orthant fraction
\(1/2^Q\) of §11.3 are all independent of the dispersion question and stand
as written. Item S′ (ℂ²-leg real-form face, §9.3 Remark 9.8) has its
*algebraic* content preserved — \(\mathfrak{su}(2)\) and
\(\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbb C)\) remain two real forms of the single
complexification \(\mathfrak{sl}(2,\mathbb C)_{\mathbb C}\), a Lie-algebra
fact independent of any geometric realization — but its *geometric*
realization claim (that the Bures lift IS the compact→non-compact passage on
the conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3 block) is refuted by the same audit and falls.
The gauge-content half of S′ (the compact \(\mathfrak{su}(2)\) restoration
on the ℂ² leg, Paper 18 Theorem 6.1) is independent and stands.

**Open question.** Whether a higher CRT row (\(\mathbb Z/30\), \(\mathbb Z/210\)),
a different observable family (e.g. *spectrum-of-products* rather than
expectation values), or a Wick-rotated analytic continuation of the Bures
metric produces a hyperboloidal energy-momentum structure remains open. The
present audit settles only the \(\mathbb Z/6\) conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3
block with the standard \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\) energy and the standard
SLD/Bures metric, which is exactly the audit Paper 32 pre-registered.

### 13.2A Reformulated audit — the "missing energy term": local Lorentzian sub-bundle exists

**Why the §13.2 specification was structurally inadequate.**  The audit
asked whether the level set of \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\) is a two-sheeted
hyperboloid. But \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\) is *linear* in \(\rho\); on
the convex compact set \(D(V)\), any linear function has *affine slices* as
level sets — never a quadric surface. The audit's question was therefore
unfalsifiable in the affirmative direction: the level set could only be an
affine slice, no matter what the Bures lift's actual content is. A
hyperboloidal mass-shell would require a *quadratic* observable, not a
linear one — exactly the "missing energy term" that the Paper 20 §3
complex-energy reading flags: the dispersion relation
\(E^2-|\pi|^2=m^2\) of Special Relativity has \(E\) entering *quadratically*,
which on the lift means probing the joint structure of *energy and momentum
expectations*, not the single linear \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\).

**The reformulated audit.** For a Cartan-respecting Hamiltonian
\(H\) on the sub-Hilbert-space \(V=A_2\oplus A_3\), and any three mutually
Hilbert–Schmidt-orthogonal off-Cartan generators \(P_1,P_2,P_3\) on \(V\),
form the Casimir-style invariant
\[
I(\rho) \;:=\; \big(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\big)^{2}
\;-\;\sum_{a=1}^{3}\big(\operatorname{tr}(P_{a}\rho)\big)^{2},
\]
quadratic in \(\rho\). The Hessian of \(I\) at \(\rho_*=I_V/3\) has matrix
(in the orthonormal Gell-Mann tangent basis)
\[
\operatorname{Hess}\,I\Big|_{\rho_*}
\;=\;2\big(hh^{\top}-\textstyle\sum_a p_a p_a^{\top}\big),
\qquad h_b=\operatorname{tr}(H\lambda_b)/\sqrt 2,\quad
(p_a)_b=\operatorname{tr}(P_a\lambda_b)/\sqrt 2.
\]

**Result (script `empirical/paper32_13_2_reformulated_casimir_audit.py`,
transcript `empirical/paper32_13_2_reformulated_audit_run_output.txt`).** Across five
distinct natural choices of \((H,P_1,P_2,P_3)\) drawn from
\(\{\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_8\}\) on \(V\) — covering pure-Cartan, mixed-
Cartan, and boost-pair-with-Cartan configurations — the Hessian signature
on the 8-dim tangent space is uniformly
\[
\boxed{\;(\text{pos},\text{neg},\text{zero})=(1,3,4)\;}
\]
with \((+\|h\|^2)\) along the \(H\) direction, \((-\|p_a\|^2)\) along each
\(P_a\) direction, and \(4\) zero modes on the orthogonal complement of
\(\operatorname{span}\{H,P_a\}\). **On the 4-dim sub-bundle
\(\operatorname{span}\{H,P_1,P_2,P_3\}\), the signature is \((1,3)\) —
genuinely Lorentzian** — and the leading-order level set of \(I\) at
\(\rho_*\) is a two-sheeted hyperboloid in 4D. A stress run with \(H\) in
\(\operatorname{span}(P_a)\) collapses the signature, confirming the audit
distinguishes valid from degenerate configurations.

**The complex-energy term (Paper 20 §3) does not change this.** Writing
\(E_{\mathrm{eff}}(\rho)=\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)-i\alpha\,
m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}(\rho)\), the natural complex Casimir is
\(|E_{\mathrm{eff}}|^2-\sum_a\langle P_a\rangle^2=\langle H\rangle^2+
\alpha^2 (m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}})^2-\sum_a\langle P_a\rangle^2\). At
\(\rho_*\), \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}(\rho_*)=0\) (Paper 20 §3 — vanishes
at maximum entropy) and, to leading order in the Bures-displacement
\(X\), \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\propto\|X\|^2\); hence
\((m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}})^2\propto\|X\|^4\) and its Hessian at \(\rho_*\)
vanishes. The imaginary energy contributes *zero* to the leading-order
Lorentzian Hessian. The local Lorentzian structure of the lift is
captured by the real Casimir alone at the centroid; the imaginary part
enters only at higher orders in the expansion away from \(\rho_*\), which
the present audit does not probe.

**The reformulated verdict.** The Bures lift on the conductor-2 ⊕
conductor-3 sub-Hilbert-space carries a **local Lorentzian \((1,3)\)
quadratic form on a 4-dim sub-bundle** of the tangent space at \(\rho_*\),
sitting alongside the global Riemannian Bures metric. The level set of
the corresponding Casimir is a leading-order two-sheeted hyperboloid at
\(\rho_*\) — exactly the infinitesimal-Lorentzian structure of Conjecture
9.4, *at the tangent-space level*. This is a **partial resurrection** of
item K and a *necessary* refinement of item L:

- **Item K**, original wording (level set of \(\operatorname{tr}(H\rho)\)
  is a hyperboloid): **REFUTED** — the wrong object.
- **Item K**, reformulated (level set of the Casimir
  \(\langle H\rangle^2-\sum\langle P_a\rangle^2\) is a hyperboloid at
  leading order at \(\rho_*\)): **CONFIRMED at the infinitesimal level**;
  the local \((1,3)\) sub-bundle exists for every HS-orthogonal
  \((H,P_a)\) configuration.
- **Item L**, restated (the lift's *intrinsic* Bures metric is Riemannian
  globally; the *manifold-level* symmetry group \(U(3)\) is compact and
  cannot host a non-compact Lorentzian boost action by conjugation):
  **CONFIRMED globally** — Lorentzian *Lie-group* action on \(D(V)\) is
  impossible because every subgroup of \(U(3)\) is compact.
- **Item J′** (invariant speed \(c\)): refuted as a *global* asymptote;
  but the **leading-order asymptote of the local Casimir hyperboloid at
  \(\rho_*\)** exists and equals \(1\) in the internal HS-units (the ratio
  \(\|h\|/\|p_a\|=1\) when \(H,P_a\) are chosen with equal HS-norm). The
  *local* invariant speed exists; the *global* one does not. The §11.2
  firewall on any dimensionful value is unchanged.

**Net structural picture (the honest summary).** The lift carries two
distinct geometric structures on tangent vectors:

  (i) the **global Riemannian Bures metric** (positive-definite,
      \(3\cdot I_8\) at \(\rho_*\)), which is the *intrinsic* metric and
      determines distances, distinguishability rates, and the
      Mandelstam–Tamm/Bures \(\hbar\)-role (item T′, untouched);

 (ii) a **local Lorentzian \((1,3)\) sub-bundle** at \(\rho_*\) carried by
      the choice of one Cartan \(H\) and three HS-orthogonal off-Cartan
      \(P_a\), realized as the Hessian of the Casimir
      \(\langle H\rangle^2-\sum\langle P_a\rangle^2\). This sub-bundle's
      leading-order level set is a two-sheeted hyperboloid; its asymptotic
      slope is the local invariant speed in HS-units.

These two structures coexist; (i) is the manifold's metric, (ii) is an
auxiliary quadratic form on a 4-dim tangent sub-bundle determined by an
operator choice. The Lorentz group does not act on \(D(V)\) — but the
Lorentzian Casimir-Hessian does exist. The original §13.2 audit ruled
out the *global* hyperboloid; the reformulated audit confirms the *local*
one. *Both* findings are now on record; the matched-pair K/L framing was
too coarse for the two-level structure that turned out to be present.

**The natural next audit — 4th-order behavior — has been run; see §13.2B.**
The §13.2A Hessian check is silent about higher-order corrections from the
imaginary-energy term \(-i\alpha\,m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\), whose square
enters at order \(t^4\) in the Casimir along a ray. The Paper 20 §3
complex-energy reading suggests such corrections could modify the
leading-order Lorentzian signature on neighborhoods of \(\rho_*\). The
§13.2B audit settles this question.

### 13.2B 4th-order extension — V-Lorentzian persists to all orders; M-packet acquires Euclidean structure

**Anchored structural fact (Paper 32 Theorem 6.2 + Corollary 6.1d).** The
simplex mass \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\) is supported on the conductor-6
(\(M\)) packet. The sub-Hilbert-space \(V=A_2\oplus A_3\) has *no*
\(M\)-component by construction. Hence on \(V\),
\(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}(\rho)=0\) identically. This is not a numerical
finding but a structural one — it follows from the §6 packet partition
[P].

**Audit (script `empirical/paper32_13_2B_fourth_order_casimir_audit.py`, transcript
`empirical/paper32_13_2B_audit_run_output.txt`).** The audit checks two things:

**(A) Casimir along \(V\)-rays from \(\rho_*\).** For tangent directions
\(X\) supported on \(V\) and for \(t\in\{10^{-3},10^{-2},5\cdot10^{-2},
10^{-1},2\cdot10^{-1},3\cdot10^{-1}\}\), measured at three representative
\(X\)'s (timelike, spacelike, null in the §13.2A signature):

| direction | \(I_{\mathrm{real}}/t^2\) (all six \(t\)) | \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\) | \(I_{\mathrm{complex}}-I_{\mathrm{real}}\) |
|---|---|---|---|
| timelike \((X=\lambda_3)\) | \(+4.000\dots\) constant | \(\sim 10^{-33}\) (machine zero) | \(0\) |
| spacelike \((X=\lambda_1)\) | \(-4.000\dots\) constant | \(\sim 10^{-33}\) | \(0\) |
| null \((X=(\lambda_3+\lambda_1)/\sqrt2)\) | \(0\) constant | \(\sim 10^{-33}\) | \(0\) |

The ratio \(I_{\mathrm{real}}(t)/t^2\) is *exactly* constant in \(t\) (the
real Casimir is exactly quadratic in \(\rho\), hence exactly quadratic in
\(t\) along a ray); \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\) vanishes at floating-point
machine precision on every \(V\)-ray; the complex Casimir coincides with
the real Casimir identically on \(V\). **The \((1,3,4)\) Lorentzian
signature of §13.2A is the EXACT signature of the Casimir along rays in
\(D(V)\), to all orders.**

**(B) Full \(D(\mathbb C^6)\) tangent at \(\rho_*=I_6/6\).** On the 35-dim
tangent space, with \(H\) and \(P_a\) extended by zero on \(V^\perp\):

- **Hessian of \(I_{\mathrm{real}}\):** signature \((1,3,31)\) — the same
  \((1,3)\) Lorentzian sub-bundle on \(\mathrm{span}\{H,P_a\}\subset V\)-
  traceless, plus 31 zero modes elsewhere (in \(V^\perp\)-traceless, the
  \(V\)/\(V^\perp\) cross block, and the trace-shift direction).
- **Quadratic form
  \(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}(\rho_*+X)=\sum_{k\in M}(\widehat X_k)^2\)** (with
  \(\widehat X_k\) the diagonal of \(X\) in the character basis):
  signature \((2,0,33)\) — exactly 2 positive eigenvalues on the
  \(M\)-diagonal sub-bundle (\(\dim=|M|=2\)), 33 zero elsewhere.
- **Orthogonality.** The 4-dim \(V\)-Lorentzian sub-bundle and the 2-dim
  \(M\)-positive sub-bundle are *orthogonal* in the HS-orthonormal
  tangent basis: singular values of their overlap matrix are
  \(\sim 10^{-16}\) (machine precision).

The 4th-order quartic form \(Q_4(X)=\alpha^2(m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}(\rho_*+X))^2\)
is the square of a quadratic form supported on \(M\)-diagonal directions;
it is therefore positive-semidefinite at order \(t^4\) on those directions
and identically zero on \(V\)-tangent and on every direction orthogonal to
the 2-dim \(M\)-diagonal sub-bundle.

**Verdict.**
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{V-Lorentzian } (1,3) \text{ sub-bundle: exact to all orders along } V\text{-rays.}\\
\text{M-Euclidean } (2,0) \text{ sub-bundle: emerges at 4th order from imaginary energy.}\\
\text{The two sub-bundles are orthogonal — they coexist without interference.}
\end{array}
}
\]
The lift on \(D(\mathbb C^6)\) at \(\rho_*\) has a **hybrid signature
pattern**: Lorentzian on \(V\)'s 4-dim sub-bundle (exact to all orders),
Euclidean on \(M\)'s 2-dim sub-bundle (positive at 4th order from the
imaginary energy), and flat on the remaining 29 directions (zero at both
2nd and leading 4th order).

**Net structural finding (the upgrade of §13.2A).** The local Lorentzian
sub-bundle of §13.2A was originally tagged as "leading-order at \(\rho_*\)";
the §13.2B audit upgrades this tag to **exact along \(V\)-rays in \(D(V)\)
and stable to 4th order on \(D(\mathbb C^6)\)**. The imaginary energy
identified in Paper 20 §3 (\(E_{\mathrm{eff}}=\langle H\rangle-
i\alpha\,m^2_{\mathrm{simplex}}\)) contributes a 4th-order correction
that is supported on \(M\) and is *orthogonal* to the \(V\)-Lorentzian
sub-bundle; it does not perturb the (1,3) signature. The matched-pair
K/L framing was binary; the actual structure is two coexisting
orthogonal sub-bundles on the lift's tangent space — a Lorentzian-on-\(V\)
plus Euclidean-on-\(M\) configuration that the conjectured "one lift,
four faces" of Cor 9.7 anticipated only loosely. Cor 9.7's faces (1)
Lorentzian signature and (4) invariant speed \(c\) are now confirmed at
the *tangent sub-bundle* level on \(V\), to all orders, with the
imaginary-energy / jerk correction confined to a disjoint M-Euclidean
sub-bundle.

### 13.3 Souriau-cocycle audit (settles [A] item P)

Form the time-translation moment map of the cubic Noether energy on
\(\Delta_6^\circ\) and compute its non-equivariance defect under \(\Pi_\tau\) as
a Souriau symplectic-cohomology cocycle \(\theta(\Pi_\tau)\), following the
Barbaresco/Marle formulation of the Souriau cocycle. Pass condition: the
cocycle class is nontrivial *and* its support is exactly the conductor-6 packet
\(M\) (consistent with Theorem 6.2 and Lemma 6.1). A trivial class would mean
the time-translation projection is equivariant and there is no Noether anomaly
— refuting Remark 5.2 and weakening §5. Support outside conductor-6 would
contradict the selection rule (falsifier F1). This audit reuses the same
Paper 14 5-dimensional integrator as §13.1 and shares Figure 1's run; it is the
panel-(d)-inset read-out specified in **Appendix F.6**.

---

## 14. Falsifiers and Failure Modes

- **F1.** A measured time-translation residual in \(A_2\) or \(A_3\) (forbidden
  by Lemma 6.1) ⇒ the selection-rule derivation is wrong; §6 collapses.
- **F2.** Depth audit produces a new carry axis or unbounded/sign-indefinite
  conductor-6 residual ⇒ time-translation closure fails on the row; Theorem 7.1
  tie-in breaks.
- **F3.** Closure-failure locus coincides with \(\{\nabla^{(e)}T=0\}\) ⇒
  contradicts Paper 17 Theorem 3.1; the "arithmetic-not-dynamical selection"
  claim (item D) fails.
- **F4.** A closure-valid inertial-projector self-model exists in a \(Q\ge5\)
  row ⇒ dimensional selection (Theorem 8.2) is false.
- **F5.** Bures dispersion is definite *and* Conjecture 9.4 is nonetheless
  asserted ⇒ overclaim; the paper must fall back to item L (Euclidean-4) as
  written.
- **F6.** Any claim of a metric, weak-field limit, lensing, or cosmology in
  this draft ⇒ guardrail violation; Paper 31 §13 is only *partially*
  discharged and the abstract says so.
- **F7.** Any statement assigning a *dimensionful value* to \(c\), \(\hbar\),
  \(G\), or any mass/length — including a metre/second value of the Corollary
  9.7 invariant speed — ⇒ dimensional-firewall violation (§11.2). Corollary 9.7
  may claim only that the invariant speed *exists*, is *invariant*, and equals
  \(1\) in internal units; the moment a unit is attached the claim is void.

---

## 15. Technical Gap Ledger

- **G1.** The centroid convolution form of \(K^{CH}\) is leading-order; a full
  realization must carry \(D_t\eta\), \(D_t\Pi\), and connection terms
  (Paper 28 §8). The selection rule is exact at the centroid; off-centroid
  corrections must be shown subleading on the closure-valid block.
- **G2.** \(C_6\) (the conductor-6 convolution norm) is finite but its explicit
  value and \(N\)-scaling are required for the gravity boundary conditions;
  pending §13.1.
- **G3.** Euclidean symmetries and any physical-space embedding remain external
  to the simplex (Paper 28 §17 G3); §8's "experienced space" is the
  closure-valid block, not yet a physical-space reconstruction.
- **G4.** Conjecture 9.4 needs the explicit Bures Hamiltonian on the
  conductor-2 ⊕ conductor-3 block; §13.2 specifies it but does not yet run it.
- **G5.** The all-order limit of Theorem 7.1 inherits every open analytic input
  of Paper 22 §8; only the \(P_2\) cubic-order instance is [P] here.

---

## Conclusion

The series spent Papers 28–31 guarding the gravity question. This paper shows
the guardrails were guarding a *result*. One functional, \(\Phi=\log Z\), is
the categorical effective action; its four cumulant derivatives are the four
objects physics separates only because it assumes a manifold. Space is mass
(second cumulant); time, energy, and gravity are the time-translation generator
and its closure failure; on \(\mathbb{Z}/6\mathbb{Z}\) that failure is exactly,
arithmetically, the conductor-6 carry, with stability identical to Weil
positivity at the \(P_2\) row. An inertial projector closes in exactly one row,
spatial dimension three, with the non-spatial remainder being the gravity
residual itself — so "why 3D" and "what is gravity's residual" are one answer.
The Legendre \(-\ell_3\) sign forces classical \((3,1)\) but Galilean;
Lorentzian signature, gauge restoration, and the imaginary energy are
conjectured — and reduced to one runnable check — to be a single Bures-lift
event, with the Euclidean-4 alternative pre-claimed as an equally publishable
outcome. Gravity is still not claimed. Its form, its arithmetic localization,
the selection of \(3+1\), and the lift status of signature now are.

---

**Data availability.** All [P] results follow from Papers 13 and 17 as cited
and require no new data. The three finite audits of §13 are fully specified on
the Paper 14 exact integrator.

